hadiths[605]{hadithnumber,text}:
"8471","From al-Aslami, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Suhayl, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas: he said: I asked Aishah: did you see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) eat rabbit? She said: I did not see him eat it, except that it was gifted to us while I was sleeping; he set aside for me the rump of it; when I woke he gave it to me and I ate it."
"8472","From Ibrahim ibn Umar, from Abd al-Karim ibn Abi Umayyah, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah: that a man narrated to him, from Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari: that a Bedouin came with a rabbit he had struck, or slaughtered with a sharp stone, saying: O Messenger of Allah! I struck it, and there is some blood on it; I see it menstruates. He said: eat. They said: what prevents you from it? He said: I am fasting. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the Bedouin: if you must fast, then fast three days of every month, and make them the white ones."
"8473","Abd al-Karim said: Jarir ibn Aws al-Aslami asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the rabbit, and he said: I do not eat it; I have been told it menstruates."
"8474","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: he said: some learned men disliked eating the kite and the crow, since the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) named them among the vicious creatures that are killed in the Haram."
"8475","From Ibrahim ibn Umar, from Abd al-Karim ibn Abi Umayyah, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr: that he disliked eating crow."
"8476","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: he said: disliked among birds is what eats carrion."
"8477","From Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim: that he disliked among birds everything that eats dead meat."
"8478","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, from Abu Tha'labah al-Khushani: he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade eating every fanged beast of prey."
"8479","From Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr: he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade, on the day of Khaybar, approaching pregnant women, selling spoils until divided, eating every fanged beast of prey, and the meat of domestic donkeys."
"8480","From Muhammad ibn Rashid: that he heard Makhul say: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade, on the day of Khaybar, eating every taloned bird, eating every fanged beast of prey, the meat of domestic donkeys, approaching pregnant women, and selling spoils until divided."
"8481","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah, from a man, from Ibn Abbas: he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade eating every fanged beast of prey, and eating every taloned bird."
"8482","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad: he said: Aishah was asked about eating every fanged beast of prey, and she recited: Say: I find not in what is revealed to me forbidden to one who eats it, except it be a carcass or blood poured forth. She said: we see the yellowness of blood in the pot."
"8483","From Ja'far ibn Sulayman, from Juwaybir, from al-Dahhak: he said: Ibn Abbas recited this verse: Say: I find not; and he said: what is besides this is lawful."
"8484","From Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad: that Nafi' informed him: he said: Ibn Umar bought jallalah camels, sent them to the reserve, they grazed until good, then he carried upon them to the Hajj."
"8485","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: that he disliked riding the jallalah, or performing Hajj upon it."
"8486","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus: he said: Amr ibn Shu'ayb informed me: he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the meat of jallalah camels and their milk, and he used to dislike performing Hajj upon them."
"8487","From al-Thawri, from Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir, from Mujahid: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the meat of the jallalah and their milk. From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibrahim ibn Abi Hurrah, from Mujahid, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the same."
"8488","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Yazid, from his father: he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab came to Makkah, and was told that a freedman of Amr ibn al-As called Najdah drove jallalah camels; he sent ordering them taken out of Makkah. He said: we cut wood upon them and carry upon them. He said: do not perform Hajj upon them, nor Umrah."
"8489","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Farrukh: he said: a man said to Ibn Umar: I wish to accompany you. He said: do not accompany me upon a jallalah."
"8490","From Abd Allah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: that he used to confine a chicken three days when he wished to eat its eggs."
"8491","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid: he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the meat of the jallalah."
"8492","And from Mujahid: he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade eating al-masburah, drinking from the mouth of the waterskin, and the meat of jallalah camels, in the year of the Conquest."
"8493","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Anas ibn Malik: that the crier of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called out: Allah and His Messenger forbid you the meat of donkeys, for it is filth — meaning domestic donkeys."
"8494","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abd Allah and Hasan, sons of Muhammad ibn Ali, from their father: that he heard his father Ali say: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade, on the day of Khaybar, the meat of tame donkeys."
"8495","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr: he said: I mentioned to him a hadith narrated to me by Abd Allah ibn Abi Awfa about the meat of donkeys, and Sa'id said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade it absolutely."
"8496","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani and Abu Ishaq al-Hajari: they said: we heard Ibn Abi Awfa say: on the day of Khaybar we found donkeys outside the village and slaughtered them; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade eating them. Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani said: I met Sa'id ibn Jubayr and mentioned that to him, and he said: he only forbade them because they used to eat filth."
"8497","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from a man: that he heard Salim ibn Abd Allah say: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the meat of domestic donkeys, and mut'ah of women, on the day of Khaybar."
"8498","Ma'mar reported, from Asim, from al-Sha'bi, from al-Bara ibn Azib: he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the meat of tame donkeys, cooked and raw."
"8499","From Isra'il, from Majza'ah ibn Zahir, from his father — and his father was of those who witnessed the Tree: he said: I was kindling fire beneath the pots with donkey meat, when the crier of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called: Allah forbids you the meat of donkeys."
"8500","From Ma'mar, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Bakr ibn Abd Allah al-Muzani: that a man of his people asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the meat of the domestic donkey, mentioning something of their condition? He said: I do not know what it is; then he permitted him."
"8501","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from one who narrated to him: that Ibn Abbas was asked about the meat of domestic donkeys, and he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only forbade them on the day of Khaybar because they were the mounts; then he recited: Say: I find not in what is revealed to me forbidden."
"8502","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Mis'ar, from Ubayd ibn Hasan, from Abd Allah ibn Ma'qil: that two men of Muzaynah asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), or one of them, mentioning that the year of drought left them nothing to feed their families, including donkeys; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: feed your family from the fat of your wealth, for I only deemed filthy for you the jallalah of the village."
"8503","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar: he said: I said to Abu al-Sha'tha: they say the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered them to overturn the pots of donkey meat. He said: al-Hakam ibn Amr al-Ghifari used to say that, but the Sea — meaning Ibn Abbas — rejected that: Say: I find not in what is revealed to me."
"8504","Ma'mar reported, from al-Zuhri: he said: I asked him about eating horse, and he said: we did not know horses to be eaten except in hardship."
"8505","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Fatimah bint al-Mundhir, from Asma bint Abi Bakr: she said: we slaughtered a horse in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and ate it."
"8506","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim: he said: one of Abd Allah's companions slaughtered a horse and they ate it, seeing no harm in it."
"8507","Ma'mar and al-Thawri reported, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ata, from Jabir: he said: we used to eat horse meat. He said: I said: mule? He said: no."
"8508","As for Ibn Jurayj, he mentioned from Ata: he said: it reached us that the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to eat horses."
"8509","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar: he said: I heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah say: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the meat of donkeys, and fed us the meat of horses."
"8510","From al-Thawri, from al-Zubayr ibn Adi, from Ibrahim: that he disliked mule meat."
"8511","Ma'mar reported: he said: I asked Qatadah about eating mule, and he said: it is but the fluid of the donkey."
"8512","Ibn Jurayj reported: he said: Ata informed me: he said: I saw the people of the mosque, the companions of Ibn al-Zubayr, eating horse and bardhawn."
"8513","He said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah say: we ate, at the time of Khaybar, horses and wild asses, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us eating the domestic donkey."
"8514","From Rabah ibn Zayd, from Ma'mar: he said: Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari informed me: he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about eating dog, and he said: a food of the Jahiliyyah; Allah has sufficed you beyond it."
"8515","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Ikrimah, freedman of Ibn Abbas: eat all that Allah, Exalted, created except three: the carcass, blood, and pork."
"8516","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: I asked Ata about the dog, and he said: it reached us that he forbids eating it."
"8517","Ibn Jurayj said: Humayd al-A'raj informed me, from Mujahid, that he deemed pardoned what was neither made lawful nor unlawful, of what Allah pardoned, except the domestic donkey and the dog."
"8518","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri: he said: the fox is a beast of prey; it is not eaten."
"8519","I think from Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, or another, from Tawus: he saw no harm in eating fox."
"8520","Ma'mar reported, from Qatadah: he said: it is not a beast of prey, and he permitted eating it."
"8521","From Ibn Jurayj: he said: I asked Ata about the hyena and the fox, and he said: eat both, for they harm; every harmful game is game."
"8522","Ma'mar reported, from Ayyub: he said: Mujahid was asked about eating monkey, and he said: it is not of the cattle."
"8523","From al-Thawri, from Ash'ath, from Ata, about a monkey killed in the Haram: he said: two just men among you shall judge concerning it."
"8524","A man of the children of Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib reported: he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id informed me: he said: I was with Ibn al-Musayyib when a man of Ghatafan came and asked him about eating waral; he said: no harm in it, and if you have something of it with you, feed us. Abd al-Razzaq said: al-waral is like the dabb."
"8525","Ma'mar reported, from Qatadah: he said: I know only that he raised it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that he forbade eating the cat and eating its price."
"8526","From Umar ibn Zayd: he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah say: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade eating the cat and eating its price."
"8527","From Ma'mar: he said: one who heard al-Hasan informed me that he disliked eating the bat and eating al-sawali. He said: I do not know whether the bat is al-sawali or not."
"8528","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar: he said: locusts at al-Rabadhah were mentioned to Umar ibn al-Khattab, and he said: I wish we had a basket or two baskets thereof."
"8529","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah: that Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn al-As said about the locust: it is but the sprinkling of the whale."
"8530","From Ma'mar, from Qatadah: Ibn Umar was asked about eating locust, and he said: wholly slaughtered — eat it all."
"8531","From Ibn Jurayj, from Muhammad ibn al-Harith ibn Sufyan, from Ali al-Azdi: that he heard Ibn Umar asked about eating locust? He said: no harm."
"8532","Ma'mar reported, from Qatadah, from Sa'id ibn Abi al-Hasan, brother of al-Hasan: he said: Allah, Blessed and Exalted, created Adam, and there remained in His hand something of his clay, so He created from it the locust; it is a host of Allah's hosts, no host more numerous than it."
"8533","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib: he said: Allah created nothing after Adam except the locust; there remained of his clay something, so He created from it the locust."
"8534","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi: he said: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the locust, and he said: a host of Allah's hosts, no host greater than it; I neither eat it nor forbid it. And he used to say: what is not forbidden is lawful for us."
"8535","From Isra'il: he said: Salim ibn Harb informed us, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas: he said: Umar used to eat locusts, saying: no harm in it, because it is not slaughtered."
"8536","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Qatadah, from Ibn al-Musayyib: he said: I saw Umar, Suhayb, and Salman eating locusts."
"8537","From al-Thawri, from Alqamah ibn Marthad, from a man he named — he said: I think he said Mughirah — from Ali: he said: the locust is like sea game."
"8538","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father: he said: in the book of Ali: locusts and fish are pure."
"8539","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu Ya'fur: that he asked Abd Allah ibn Abi Awfa about locust, and he said: I campaigned with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seven campaigns, or six campaigns, eating locusts."
"8540","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu Ya'fur, from Anas ibn Malik: he says: the wives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to gift locusts to one another in dishes."
"8541","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Shabib ibn Gharqadah, from Jundub: that he asked Ibn Abbas about locust, and he said: no harm in eating it."
"8542","From al-Thawri, from Yunus, from Ibn Hubab, from Abu Abd Allah: he said: Salman was asked about cheese, furs, and ghee, and he said: Allah's lawful is His lawful which He made lawful in the Qur'an, Allah's unlawful is what Allah forbade in the Qur'an, and what is besides that is something He pardoned."
"8543","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in his illness in which he died: people shall not hold anything against me, for I make lawful only what Allah made lawful in His Book, and I forbid only what Allah forbade in His Book."
"8544","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar: that he heard Ubayd ibn Umayr say: Allah made lawful His lawful and forbade His unlawful; what He made lawful is lawful, what He forbade is unlawful, and what He was silent about is pardon."
"8545","Ibn Jurayj reported: he said: Ata informed me, from Ubayd ibn Umayr, that he used to say: Allah made lawful and forbade; what He made lawful, make lawful, what He forbade, avoid; and He left of that things He neither forbade nor made lawful — that is pardon from Allah. Then he says: O you who believe, ask not about things."
"8546","From al-Thawri, from Jabir: he said: I asked al-Sha'bi about elephant meat, and he recited: Say: I find not in what is revealed to me forbidden."
"8547","Ma'mar reported, from one who heard al-Hasan say: the elephant is a pig; its meat is not eaten, its milk is not drunk — or he said: its udder is not milked, and its hoof is not ... ."
"8548","Al-Awza'i reported, from Wasil, from Mujahid: he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to dislike seven things of sheep: blood, vulva, testicles, gland, penis, bladder, and gallbladder; and he used to like of sheep its fore-part."
"8549","From Ma'mar, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Shurahbil: he said: nothing of sheep is forbidden except its blood."
"8550","From al-Aslami, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father: he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to dislike spleen."
"8551","From Uthman ibn Matar, from Sa'id, from Qatadah, from Khilas ibn Amr: that Ali used to dislike of sheep the spleen, of fish al-jirri, and of birds every taloned one."
"8552","From al-Thawri, from Fitr, from Abu Ya'la, from Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah: he said: I asked him about spleen and jirri, and he recited this verse: Say: I find not in what is revealed to me forbidden."
"8553","Ma'mar reported: he said: Hisham ibn Urwah informed me, from his father, from Zayd ibn Thabit: he said: I eat spleen, though I have no need of it, but to show my family that there is no harm in it."
"8554","Ibn Uyaynah reported, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq: he said: I said to Abu Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn Ali: it reached him that Ali would not eat jirrith meat, nor enter a house with an image, nor eat spleen. He said: as for spleen, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) deemed it filthy and did not eat it, saying: it is but a gathering of blood. So Ali would not eat it. As for a house with an image, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not enter a house with an image. As for jirrith, it is a fish the People of the Book do not eat."
"8555","From al-Thawri, from Yahya ibn Abi Salih, from Amrah bint al-Tubayh al-Adawiyyah, from Ali: she said: I passed by him with a jirriyyah in a basket, its ends sticking out of the basket, and he said: for how much? I said: for a quarter of flour. Ali said: how good this is."
"8556","Al-Thawri informed us, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas: he was asked about the jirrith (a kind of eel-like fish) and said: There is no harm in it; it is only something the Jews disliked."
"8557","From his father, that Umar ibn Zayd informed him, from Amr ibn Dinar: that the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ate liver while it was dripping with fresh blood."
"8558","From Mamar, from Abu Ishaq, from a woman of Hamdan called Tamlik: that she asked Umm Salamah about eating cheese, and she said: Put the knife into it, then say: In the name of Allah, then eat."
"8559","From Mamar, from al-Amash, I think he mentioned it, from Shaqiq: that it was said to Umar: There are people who make cheese and put the rennet of dead animals in it, so Umar said: Mention the name of Allah and eat."
"8560","From Israil, from Simak ibn Harb, from a man, from Kathir ibn Shihab, who said: I asked Umar ibn al-Khattab about cheese, and he said: Mention the name of Allah and eat."
"8561","From Abu Jafar al-Razi, from al-Rabi ibn Anas, from Abu al-Aliyah, who said: They asked him about rennet, and he said: Milk does not die."
"8562","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, who said: Ibn Umar was asked about the cheese made by the Magians, and he said: Whatever I find in the market of the Muslims I buy, and I do not ask about it. Ayyub said: Nafi said: Had Ibn Umar seen of the Magians what I have seen, I would have thought he would dislike it; and Nafi had visited part of the land of Persia."
"8563","From Mamar, who said: I asked al-Zuhri about cheese, and he said: Whatever I find in the market of the Muslims I buy, and I do not ask about it."
"8564","From Israil, from Abu Ishaq, from Qurazah ibn Artah, from Abd Khayr, from Kathir ibn Shihab, who said: I asked Umar about cheese, and he said: Eat, for it is only milk or colostrum."
"8565","From Israil, who said: Isa ibn Abi Azzah informed me that he heard al-Shabi say: Say the name of Allah over cheese and clarified butter, and eat."
"8566","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Mabad, who said: Ibn Abbas used to see no harm in the cheese made by the Jews and Christians."
"8567","From Hushaym, from Abu Hibban, who said: I asked Ibn Umar about cheese, and part of his answer was to say: Nothing comes to us from Iraq more pleasing to us than cheese."
"8568","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi al-Husayn, from Ali al-Azdi, who said: Ibn Umar was asked about silk and said: We have heard that whoever wears it in this world will not wear it in the Hereafter; and I asked him about cheese, and he said: About which aspect of it are you asking me? He said: I said: They put in it, or we fear they put in it, the rennet of dead animals. He said: Leave what makes you doubt for what does not make you doubt."
"8569","From Israil, from Majzaah ibn Zahir, from Ata al-Basri, who said: I was with Ibn Umar when a man asked him about tila, meaning rubb (thickened grape syrup), and he said: The Commander of the Believers used to drink it and provide it to our servants. I said: But they cook it when it is wine. He said: If you know that it is wine, do not drink it. I said: And cheese? He said: It is brought from Iraq and we eat it and feed it to our servants. I said: But they put dead meat in it. He said: If you know that there is dead meat in it, do not eat it."
"8570","From al-Thawri, from his father, from Mundhir al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn Ali, who said: Eat cheese outright (without questioning)."
"8571","From Mamar, from a man, that he asked Said ibn al-Musayyib about cheese, and he said: If you know that there is dead meat in it, do not eat it; otherwise mention the name of Allah and eat."
"8572","From Qays ibn al-Rabi, that Amr ibn Mansur al-Hamdani informed him, from al-Shabi and al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, who said: A piece of cheese was brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the expedition of Tabuk, and it was said: O Messenger of Allah, this is a food made by the people of Persia, and I fear there may be dead meat in it. He said: Mention the name of Allah over it and eat."
"8573","Ibn Jurayj: from Asim ibn Ubaydillah ibn Asim, from Abdullah ibn Amir, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Follow up between Hajj and Umrah, for following up between them removes poverty and sins just as the bellows removes the dross of iron. From Mamar, from Ibn al-Munkadir, from one who narrated to him, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, that he said: Follow up between Hajj and Umrah, and he mentioned the like of it, but he did not raise it (to the Prophet)."
"8574","From al-Thawri, from Sumayy, from Dhakwan, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The accepted Hajj has no reward except Paradise, and the two Umrahs expiate what is between them. From Abdullah ibn Umar, who said: Sumayy narrated to me, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said the like of it: And the accepted Hajj has no reward except Paradise."
"8575","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Jabir, from Abu Hazim, freedman of the Ansar, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever performs Hajj to this House and does not commit obscenity nor disobedience will be as on the day his mother gave birth to him."
"8576","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from one who heard Umar ibn al-Khattab say: Whoever goes out to this House, roused by nothing but prayer there and touching the Stone, will have what came before it expiated."
"8577","From Ibrahim ibn Yazid, who said: Yusuf ibn Mahak informed me that Umar ibn al-Khattab went out and saw a caravan and said: Who is the caravan? They said: Pilgrims. He said: Did nothing else rouse you? three times. They said: No. He said: If the caravan knew with whom they have halted, their eyes would be comforted with bounty after forgiveness. By Him in whose Hand is the life of Umar, no camel lifts its foot nor puts it down but Allah raises for him a rank by it, removes a sin from him by it, and writes for him a good deed by it."
"8578","From Mamar, from Layth, from Mujahid, from Kab, who said: The delegations to Allah are three: the pilgrim, those performing Umrah, and those striving in jihad. Allah called them and they answered Him, and they asked Allah and He gave them."
"8579","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Abdullah ibn Damrah, from Kab, who said: When the pilgrim and the one performing Umrah say takbir - he said: I do not know whether he mentioned the warrior - the one next to him says takbir, then the one next to him, until it ends at the horizon."
"8580","From Malik, from Yahya ibn Said, from Muhammad ibn Hibban: that a man passed by Abu Dharr while he was at al-Rabadhah, and asked him: Where are you heading? He said: Hajj. He said: Did nothing else rouse you? He said: No. He said: Then start your deeds anew. The man said: So I went out until I came to Madinah, and I stayed as long as Allah willed, and then people were crowding around a man, so I pressed forward, and there was the old man I had met at al-Rabadhah, meaning Abu Dharr; and when he saw me he said: It is what I told you."
"8581","From Jafar ibn Sulayman, who said: Ata ibn al-Saib narrated to us, from Mujahid, who said: While Umar ibn al-Khattab was sitting between al-Safa and al-Marwah, a caravan arrived and halted at the gate of the mosque, and they circumambulated the House while Umar looked at them, then they went out and ran between al-Safa and al-Marwah; and when they finished he said: Bring them to me, so they were brought to him, and he said: From where are you? They said: From the people of Iraq; I think he said: They said: From the people of Kufah. He said: What brought you? They said: As pilgrims. He said: Did you not come for trade, nor inheritance, nor seeking a debt? They said: No. He said: Did you turn your backs (on home)? They said: Yes. He said: Did you exert yourselves? They said: Yes. He said: Did you go barefoot (in hardship)? They said: Yes. He said: Then start anew."
"8582","From Abdullah ibn Isa, who said: Salamah ibn Wahram informed me, from a man of the Asharis, from Abu Musa al-Ashari: that a man asked him about the pilgrim, and he said: The pilgrim intercedes for four hundred households of his people, and he is blessed in forty of the mothers of the camels that carried him, and he comes out of his sins as on the day his mother gave birth to him. He said: A man said to him: O Abu Musa, I used to strive for Hajj, but I have grown weak and old; is there anything equal to Hajj? He said to him: Can you free seventy believing slaves from the children of Ishmael? As for departing and returning, I find nothing equal to it."
"8583","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Abis ibn Rabiah, from Umar, who said: When you lay down the saddles, then bind the journey to Hajj and Umrah, for it is one of the two jihads."
"8584","From al-Thawri, from Muawiyah ibn Ishaq, from Abayah ibn Rifah, from al-Husayn ibn Ali, who said: A man asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about jihad, and he said: Shall I not guide you to a jihad with no harm in it? Hajj."
"8585","From Mamar, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, who said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I am a cowardly man; I cannot bear facing the enemy. He said: Shall I not guide you to a jihad in which there is no fighting? He said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Upon you is Hajj and Umrah."
"8586","From al-Thawri, from Muawiyah ibn Ishaq, from Aishah bint Talhah, from Aishah, who said: We asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about jihad, and he said: Hajj suffices you - or: your jihad is Hajj."
"8587","From Mamar, from Zayd ibn Aslam: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed the Farewell Hajj with his wives, then said: It is only this one, then remaining upon the mats - saying: Keep to remaining upon the mats in your houses."
"8588","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from al-Qasim ibn Abi Bazzah, who mentioned it; he said: I do not know whether he raised it or not; he said: Allah boasts to His angels about the people of Arafah, saying: Look at My servants; they have come to Me disheveled, dusty, exposed to the sun; and none is seen freed more than on that day, and no arrogant one is forgiven in it."
"8589","From Jafar ibn Sulayman, who said: Muhammad ibn Jahadah narrated to me, from Talhah al-Yami, who said: I heard him say: We used to say that whoever is sealed for him with one of three - either he said: Paradise becomes due for him, or he said: he is freed from the Fire: whoever fasts the month of Ramadan and when the month ends he dies; and whoever goes out as a pilgrim and when he returns from his Hajj he dies; and whoever goes out performing Umrah and when he returns from his Umrah he dies."
"8590","From al-Aslami, from Abu al-Huwayrith, from Amir ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Successive Hajjs and Umrahs in sequence ward off an evil death and the burden of poverty."
"8591","He said: And Khalid ibn Rabah narrated to me, from al-Muttalib ibn Hantab: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw people who had shaved their heads, and said to Umar: Ask them what roused them. They said: Umrah. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The people have departed and none of their sins followed them."
"8592","From his father, from Khallad ibn Abd al-Rahman, who said: I asked Said ibn Jubayr: Which pilgrim is best? He said: Whoever feeds food and restrains his tongue. He said: And al-Thawri informed us, who said: We have heard that it is part of the piety of Hajj."
"8593","Al-Aslami narrated to me, who said: Ibn al-Munkadir narrated to me, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: What is the piety of the pilgrim? He said: Feeding food and abandoning speech."
"8594","Al-Aslami said: And Safwan ibn Sulaym narrated to me, from Ata ibn Yasar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever performs Hajj to the House and completes its rites, and the Muslims are safe from his tongue and his hand, will be forgiven what preceded of his sin."
"8595","Mamar and others informed us, from Ayyub, who said: Umar said: No pilgrim ever became poor - saying: he never fell into poverty."
"8596","From al-Aslami, from Safwan ibn Sulaym, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Perform Hajj and you will be enriched, and go out raiding and you will be healthy."
"8597","From al-Thawri, who said: We have heard that the piety of Hajj is good food and good speech."
"8598","From Abu Hanifah, from Qays ibn Abi Bakr ibn Abi Musa, from his father, who said: While I was sitting with Ibn Abbas a man came to him and said: I have come upon perfume while in ihram, so Ibn Abbas said: I judge against you, I and Abu Bakr, a sheep. Then another came to him and said: I have completed my rites except the circumambulation, so he said: Circumambulate the House, then return to me. He said: So he returned to him and said: I have circumambulated. So Ibn Abbas said: Go and begin anew with deeds."
"8599","He informed us, who said: Bakkar informed us, who said: Tawus was asked: Is Hajj after the obligatory one better, or charity? He said: Where are the departing and the traveling, and staying awake, and toil, and circumambulation of the House, and prayer at it, and standing at Arafah and Muzdalifah and stoning the pillars? As if saying: Hajj."
"8600","From al-Thawri, when a man asked him: Is Hajj after the obligatory one better, or charity? He said: Abu Miskin informed me, from Ibrahim, that he said: When he has performed several Hajjs, then charity. And al-Hasan used to say: When he has performed one Hajj."
"8601","From Mamar, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A circumambulation of seven equals a freed slave."
"8602","From Mamar, from Hawshab, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, narrating from Abdullah ibn Amr, who said: Whoever circumambulates the House and prays two rakahs, saying nothing but good, will be like the equal of a freed slave."
"8603","From al-Thawri, from al-Ala ibn al-Musayyib, from his father, or from a man, from Abu Said al-Khudri, who said: The Lord, blessed and exalted, says: A servant for whom I have expanded provision, then he does not come to Me in every four years, is deprived."
"8604","From al-Thawri and Ibn Uyaynah, from Salim ibn Abi Hafsah, that Ibn Abbas said: If people abandoned visiting this House for a single year, they would not be rained upon."
"8605","From a shaykh of the people of Khurasan called Abu Abdullah, who said: Sulayman ibn Yasar narrated to me, from Kab: that he was asked about Bayt al-Maqdis and would inform of the merit in it, when a man of the people of Syria said: O Abu Abbas, you mention Bayt al-Maqdis much and do not mention this House much. So Kab said to him: By Him in whose Hand is the life of Kab, Allah has not created upon the back of the earth a house better than this House; it has a tongue and two lips, and they speak, and it has a heart with which it understands. A man called Abu Hafs said to him: O Abu Ishaq, will you keep narrating to us fables - that stones speak? So Kab said: By Him in whose Hand is my soul, the Kabah complained to its Lord and said: O Lord, my visitors have grown few and my frequenters few, so Allah revealed to it: I am sending down upon you a new Torah and worshipping servants, prostrating, who yearn for you as the dove yearns for its egg, and flock to you as eagles flock; whoever circumambulates you seven times will have the equal of a freed slave, and there is no one who shaves who shaves at this House but he will have for every hair a light on the Day of Resurrection."
"8606","Mamar informed us, who said: I heard a man called Ibn Abi Salamah, of the descendants of Umm Salamah, say: A man died at Mina in the last days of Tashriq, so a man came and said: O Commander of the Believers, the son of our sister has died; shall we bury him? He said: So Umar said: What prevents me from burying a man who has not sinned since he was forgiven."
"8607","From Ibn Mujahid, from his father, from Ibn Umar, who said: Two men came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), one of the Ansar and the other of Thaqif, and the Ansari preceded him. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the Thaqafite: O brother of Thaqif, the Ansari has preceded you. The Ansari said: I will let him begin, O Messenger of Allah. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O brother of Thaqif, ask about your need, and if you wish I will inform you of what you came to ask about. He said: That you do so is more pleasing to me. He said: You came to ask about your prayer, and about your bowing, and about your prostration, and about your fasting, saying: What is there for me in it? He said: Yes, by Him who sent you with the truth. He said: Then pray the first of the night and its last, and sleep its middle. He said: And if you pray its middle, then you are thus. He said: And when you stand for prayer and bow, place your hands upon your knees and spread your fingers, then raise your head until every limb returns to its joint; and when you prostrate, press your forehead firmly upon the ground. He said: And fast the white nights: the thirteenth, fourteenth and fifteenth. Then he turned to the Ansari and said: Ask about your need, and if you wish I will inform you. He said: That is more pleasing to me. He said: You came to ask me about your going out from your house heading for the Sacred House, saying: What is there for me in it? And you came to ask about your standing at Arafah, saying: What is there for me in it? And about your stoning the pillars, saying: What is there for me in it? He said: Yes, by Him who sent you with the truth. He said: As for your going out from your house heading for the Sacred House, then for every step your mount treads, Allah writes for you a good deed and erases from you an evil deed. And as for your standing at Arafah, then Allah, blessed and exalted, descends to the heaven of the world and boasts of them to the angels, saying: These are My servants; they have come disheveled and dusty from every deep mountain pass, hoping for My mercy and fearing My punishment, though they have not seen Me - so how would it be if they had seen Me. Then if there were upon you sins like the sands of Alij, or like the days of the world, or like the drops of the sky, Allah would wash them from you. And as for your stoning the pillars, it is stored for you. And as for your shaving your head, then for every hair that falls you have a good deed. And when you circumambulate the House, you come out of your sins as on the day your mother gave birth to you."
"8608","From one who heard Qatadah say: Khilas ibn Amr narrated to us, from Ubadah ibn al-Samit, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the Day of Arafah: O people, Allah has bestowed upon you in this day, so He forgives you except for claims between you; and He has gifted your offender to your doer of good, and given your doer of good what he asked. Proceed in the name of Allah. And when he was at Muzdalifah, he said: Allah has forgiven your righteous, and your righteous has interceded for your wicked; forgiveness descends and covers them all, then forgiveness is dispersed in the lands and falls upon every repentant one who guarded his tongue and his hand. And Iblis and his soldiers are upon the mountains of Arafat looking at what Allah has done with them; and when forgiveness descends he and his soldiers call out woe, saying: I had roused them for ages of time, then forgiveness came and covered them; so they disperse calling out woe and ruin."
"8609","From Malik, from Ibrahim ibn Abi Ablah, from Talhah ibn Ubaydillah ibn Kariz, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no day on which Iblis is more driven away, nor more crushed, nor more enraged, than the Day of Arafah, because of what he sees of mercy descending and Allah passing over grave matters - except what he saw on the Day of Badr. It was said: And what did he see on the Day of Badr? He said: He saw Gabriel arranging the angels."
"8610","Ibn Muharrar informed us, who said: I heard Ata ibn Abi Rabah narrating from Aishah: that she asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about a man who performed Hajj much: should he put his spending in kinship or in freeing? So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A circumambulation of seven with no idle talk in it equals a freed slave."
"8611","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Muhammad ibn Suqah, who said: I heard Said ibn Jubayr say: Whoever heads for this House seeking worldly life or the Hereafter will be given it."
"8612","From Mamar, from Qatadah, from Kab: that this House complained of ruin to Allah, when a man said to him: Does it have a tongue with which it speaks? He said: Yes, and a heart with which it understands. He said: I shall replace for you with a Torah, and make for you dwellers who incline to you as the foster-mother inclines to her chicks, and flock to you as eagles flock to their nests, prostrating."
"8613","Mamar informed us, from al-Amash, from Mujahid, who said: A man said in the presence of Ibn Umar: How many are the pilgrims? So Ibn Umar said: How few are they? He said: Then Ibn Umar saw a man upon a camel on a shabby saddle whose bridle was a rope, and said: Perhaps this one."
"8614","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu Abdullah, from Said ibn Jubayr, who said: I heard Shurayh the Iraqi say: Pilgrims are few, and riders are many."
"8615","From Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr, who said: I heard al-Muthanna say: I heard Tawus say: I was sitting with Jabir ibn Abdullah when a group of the people of Yemen passed by him, worn out from water and firewood, and Jabir ibn Abdullah said: I have seen none more like us with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) than these."
"8616","From al-Thawri, from Fudayl ibn Marzuq, from Adi ibn Thabit, from Abu Hazim, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah is Good and accepts only what is good. He commanded the believers with what He commanded the messengers, saying: O messengers, eat of the good things and do righteousness; then He said: O you who believe, eat of the good things We have provided you. He said: Then he mentioned a man who prolongs travel, disheveled and dusty, stretching his hand to the sky, saying: O Lord, O Lord, while his food is unlawful, and his clothing unlawful, and he was nourished in the unlawful - so how will he be answered."
"8617","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, who said: Four things in four are not accepted: neither in Hajj, nor Umrah, nor jihad, nor charity: treachery, theft, misappropriation of spoils, and the wealth of the orphan."
"8618","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar: that a man asked him: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, a man employed over the charities took some of it and performed Hajj with it. So Ibn Umar said: What if a man stole the goods of the pilgrims and performed Hajj with them? The man said: May Allah forgive, O Abu Abd al-Rahman. So Ibn Umar said: These charities belong to the poor."
"8619","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ismail ibn Muhammad ibn Sad informed me that Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf informed him that al-Saib ibn Yazid informed him that he heard al-Ala ibn al-Hadrami say: He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The stay of the Emigrant in Makkah after completing his rites is three."
"8620","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Humayd, who said: I heard Ibn Abd al-Aziz asking his companions while he was governor at Madinah: What have you heard about staying in Makkah? So al-Saib ibn Yazid said to him: Al-Ala ibn al-Hadrami said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The stay of the Emigrant after completing his rites is three."
"8621","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Musa ibn Abi Isa, who said: When Umar ibn al-Khattab came to Makkah and completed his rites, he would say: You are not an abode of staying nor residence."
"8622","Hisham informed us, from al-Hasan and Muhammad, who both said: The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would perform Hajj, then return, and perform Umrah, and would not reside in seclusion."
"8623","From Aflah ibn Humayd, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, who said: When he performed Umrah he would stay three."
"8624","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, who said: Going back and forth to Makkah was dearer to them than residing there, and when they performed Umrah they liked to stay three."
"8625","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Muhammad ibn Qays, who said: I heard al-Shabi say: For me to stay at Liham Ayun is dearer to me than to stay in Makkah."
"8626","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah, who said: I heard al-Shabi dislike residing in Makkah. Zakariyya said: So I asked Jabir: Why does Amir dislike residing in Makkah? He said: Because of the letter of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Khuzaah: whoever of you stays in the place of emigration may dwell only in Hajj or Umrah."
"8627","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Ata, who said: I saw Jabir ibn Abdullah, and Ibn Umar, and Abu Hurayrah - and I know he mentioned Abu Said al-Khudri - performing Hajj, then residing in seclusion, and performing Umrah and Hajj."
"8628","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Fitr, from Abu al-Tufayl, who said: Muhammad ibn Ali said to me: Stay in this land, meaning Makkah, even if you eat idah (a thorny tree) or the leaves of trees."
"8629","From Ibn Uyaynah, from a shaykh of the people of Makkah, from his father: that Abu Dharr came performing Umrah and stayed with them, and stayed three, then went out."
"8630","From Said ibn Hammad ibn Uthman, from Abu Sulayman, who said: Said ibn al-Musayyib said to me: Do not live in Makkah - and Uthman was a handsome man. He said: So I thought he meant that, and I said: O Abu Muhammad, I hope Allah will protect me. He said: I do not mean that, but when you dwell in the Sanctuary you will soon do in it what is done outside the Sanctuary, when it grows long upon you, and error in it is greater."
"8631","From Hisham ibn Hassan, who said: Rings were sent with me from Basra for the House, and they were lost, so I asked al-Qasim ibn Muhammad: Is there anything due in them? He said: No. Then he said: And what do they do with gifts to the House? For me to give a dirham in charity is dearer to me than to gift a hundred thousand to this House; and even if wealth flowed over this valley, I would not gift any of it to the House."
"8632","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Yahya ibn Said informed me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from al-Qasim: that Aishah said: For me to give a dirham in charity is dearer to me than to gift to the Kabah such-and-such, for something she had heard."
"8633","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Abd al-Karim informed me that Ibrahim ibn Abi Bakr, or another, informed him, who said: I sat with Ibn Umar and greeted, intending to ask him about gifts to the House. He said: I think he said: I have vowed to gift to it - when a man asked him about that, and he said: And what does the House do with that? He said: I have done it. He said: Then fulfill what you said. He said: I said: And I too, O Abu Abd al-Rahman? He said: And you too. He said: I said: Yes. He said: Then fulfill. And he had disapproved of that for both of them, yet ordered them to fulfill it."
"8634","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from his wife, who said: I was with Aishah when she was asked about a man who gifted something to the House, and she said: Let them put it among the poor, for this House is maintained from the wealth of Allah."
"8635","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, who said: When you gift something to the House, put it into the perfume with which it is perfumed."
"8636","From Ibn Jurayj, from al-Hasan ibn Muslim, from Safiyyah bint Shaybah, from Aishah: that she used to circumambulate the House while veiled."
"8637","From al-Thawri, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Rafi, from Jabir ibn Zayd, who disliked that a woman circumambulate the House while veiled. And Sufyan holds to the word of Aishah, and he mentioned the hadith of Ibn Jurayj, from al-Hasan ibn Muslim."
"8638","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Layth, from Tawus, who disliked that she circumambulate the House while veiled."
"8639","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I used to hear my father claim that Ibrahim was the first to set up the landmarks of the Sanctuary."
"8640","From Mamar, from Ibn Khuthaym, from Muhammad ibn al-Aswad, who said: Ibrahim was the first to set up the landmarks of the Sanctuary."
"8641","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Khuthaym, from Muhammad ibn al-Aswad, that he informed him: that Ibrahim the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was the first to set up the landmarks of the Sanctuary, and Gabriel pointed to him their places."
"8642","Ibn Jurayj said: And he informed me from him also: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered on the day of the Conquest Tamim ibn Asad, grandfather of Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Muttalib ibn Tamim, and he renewed them."
"8643","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: The people of ignorance would not commit anything in the Sanctuary but it was hastened for them; then there has been of the matter what you have seen; then it is near that none will commit anything of it but it will be hastened for him, until if a black slave-girl sought refuge in it, none would touch her."
"8644","From Mamar, from Ibn Khuthaym, who said: Abu Nujayh informed me, from Huwaytib ibn Abd al-Uzza: that a slave-girl in the ignorance sought refuge in the House, so her mistress came and pulled her, and her hand was paralyzed. He said: And Islam came while her hand was paralyzed."
"8645","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Misar, from Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Sabit, who said: The forearm of a woman gleamed while she was circumambulating the House in the ignorance, so a man placed his hand upon her forearm, and his hand stuck to her hand. Then a man came and said: Go to the place in which you did it, and covenant with the Lord of this House that you will not return. He said: So he did, and he was released."
"8646","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood at al-Hazwarah and said: I have known that you are the best of the land of Allah, and the most beloved land to Allah; and were it not that your people drove me out, I would not have come out. From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Our elders heard that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I have known that you are the best of the lands of Allah, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Mamar."
"8647","From Mamar, who said: Abd al-Karim al-Jazari informed me that he heard Mujahid say: I saw Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As at Arafah, with his dwelling in the outer land and his prayer-place in the Sanctuary. It was said to him: Why do you do this? He said: Because deeds in it are better, and sin in it is greater."
"8648","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ismail ibn Umayyah informed me that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: For me to err seventy errors at Rukbah is dearer to me than to err a single error at Makkah."
"8649","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: And Mujahid said: Umar warned Quraysh - and there were three Arab tribes there who perished - : For me to err twelve errors at Rukbah is dearer to me than to err a single error toward its corner."
"8650","From Mamar, who said: One who heard Abu al-Tufayl informed me, saying: The House is the counterpoise of the Throne of Allah; if the Frequented House fell, it would fall upon it; and it is the navel of the earth, and from it it was spread."
"8651","From al-Aslami, from Safwan ibn Sulaym, from Kurayb, freedman of Ibn Abbas: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Frequented House which is in the heaven is called al-Durah; it is above the Sacred House - if it fell it would fall upon it. Seventy thousand angels tend it every day who have never seen it; and in the seventh heaven there is a sanctuary equal to its sanctuary."
"8652","Mamar informed us, from Wahb ibn Abdullah, that Abu al-Tufayl informed him that he heard Ibn al-Kawwa ask Ali about the Frequented House, what it is. So Ali said: That is al-Durah in the seventh heaven at the Throne; seventy thousand angels enter it every day who do not return to it until the Day of Resurrection."
"8653","From Mamar, from Humayd al-Araj, from Mujahid, who said: Touching the Stone and the Corner erases sins."
"8654","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr, from his father, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that he said: Wiping the Black Stone and the Yemeni Corner casts off sins utterly."
"8655","From Ibn Jurayj, from Muhammad ibn Abbad, who said: I heard Ibn Abbas say: By Him in whose Hand is the life of Ibn Abbas, no Muslim servant aligns himself with the Corner asking Allah for good but He gives it to him."
"8656","From Bishr ibn Rafi, who said: I heard Abu Abdullah, cousin of Abu Hurayrah, narrating that he heard Abu Hurayrah say: Touching the Corner erases sins utterly."
"8657","From Bishr ibn Rafi, who said: Ismail ibn Abi Sad al-Sanani informed me that he heard Ikrimah, freedman of Ibn Abbas, from Ibn Abbas, that he heard him say: Whoever touches the Corner then supplicates will be answered. It was said to Ibn Abbas: Even if quickly? He said: Even if quicker than flashing lightning."
"8658","From Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr, from Uthman ibn al-Aswad: that Mujahid said to a man: No one places his hand upon the Yemeni Corner then supplicates but he is near to being answered; so come, let us place our hands then supplicate."
"8659","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Mujahid said: The Corner and the Station will come on the Day of Resurrection greater than Abu Qubays, each of them having two eyes, two tongues and two lips, bearing witness for whoever fulfilled them with fulfillment."
"8660","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I was told, from Salman al-Farisi: that he was sitting between Zamzam and the Corner and the Station while people crowded upon the Corner, and he said to his companions: Do you know what this is? They said: Yes, this is the Stone. He said: I know, but it is of the stones of Paradise which Allah created with His Hand; it will be gathered on the Day of Resurrection with two eyes, two lips and a tongue, bearing witness for whoever touched it in truth."
"8661","From al-Thawri, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, who said: They liked to go early to Mina, and they liked to touch the Stone when they arrived, and when they circumambulated, and when they sealed, and on the Day of Sacrifice, and on the Day of Departure."
"8662","From Hisham, from al-Hasan, who said: He liked to touch the Stone when he began and when he sealed; and if he could not do that, he would say takbir and send blessings upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and pass on."
"8663","Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman informed us, who said: I saw Said ibn Jubayr circumambulating the House, and when he aligned with the Corner without touching it, he faced it and said takbir. From Ibn al-Taymi, from Layth, from Tawus, the like of it."
"8664","From al-Thawri, from a companion of his: that Ibrahim would raise his hands when he faced the Stone."
"8665","From Hushaym, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, who said: If you are able to touch the Corner, otherwise face it and say tahlil and takbir. And he liked to begin with the Stone and seal with it: in the circumambulation in which he runs, and the circumambulation in which he exits ihram, and the circumambulation in which he departs. And he liked to crowd upon the Stone in these three: when he touches it, and begins with it, and seals with it."
"8666","From Mamar, from a man, from al-Minhal ibn Amr, from Mujahid, who said: The Station and the Stone will come on the Day of Resurrection like Abu Qubays, each of them having two eyes and two lips, calling out in their loudest voices, bearing witness for whoever fulfilled them with fulfillment."
"8667","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abd al-Karim, from Mujahid, who said: There is no harm in touching the Stone from the side of the door when you touch it with your hand."
"8668","From a man of the people of Taif, from an elder of them called Muhammad ibn Sufyan, from Fatimah bint Sufyan, who said: When Allah took the covenant from the children of Israel - or Adam - He placed it in the Corner; so part of fulfilling the covenant of Allah is touching the Stone."
"8669","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: I said to Ata: Has there reached you any word recommended at touching the Corner? He said: As if he enjoined takbir."
"8670","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi: that Ibn Umar, when he touched the Corner, would say: In the name of Allah, and Allah is Greatest. From Ibn Jurayj, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, the like of it."
"8671","From Mamar, from one who heard al-Hasan: that he, when he touched the Corner, would glorify Allah and say takbir, then say: O Allah, I seek refuge in You from disbelief, and poverty, and the stations of humiliation."
"8672","From al-Thawri, from Ubayd al-Mukatib, from Ibrahim: that he would say at touching the Stone: There is no god but Allah, and Allah is Greatest; O Allah, in affirmation of Your Book and the Sunnah of Your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"8673","From Muhammad ibn Ubaydillah, from Juwaybir, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, from Ibn Abbas: that he, when he touched, would say: O Allah, with faith in You and affirmation of Your Book and the Sunnah of Your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"8674","From one of the people of Madinah, from al-Hajjaj, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas: that he would say at touching the Stone: O Allah, in fulfillment of Your covenant, and affirmation of Your Book, and following the Sunnah of Your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"8675","From Mamar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf: What did you do, O Abu Muhammad, in touching the Stone? He said: Sometimes I touched and sometimes I left. He said: You did right."
"8676","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father: that Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf asked permission of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for Umrah, and he permitted him, and said to him: What did you do in touching the Corner? He said: Sometimes I touched and sometimes I left. He said: You did right."
"8677","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, who said: I have not left touching the two Corners in ease nor hardship since I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) touch them. And Mamar said: And Ayyub informed me, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, the like of it; and Nafi added, saying: Ibn Umar would crowd upon the Stone until his nose bled, then he would come and wash it."
"8678","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, who said: I will not leave touching these two Corners since I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) touch them. Nafi said: Ibn Umar would crowd upon the two Corners until his nose bled, then he would come and wash it."
"8679","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, who said: It was said to Tawus: Ibn Umar would not leave touching the two Yemeni Corners in every circumambulation. So Tawus said: But one better than him would leave them. It was said: Who? He said: His father."
"8680","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibrahim ibn Abi Hurrah, who said: Salim and I would crowd together for Abdullah ibn Umar upon the two Corners."
"8681","Ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Talhah ibn Ishaq ibn Talhah, who said: I asked al-Qasim ibn Muhammad about crowding upon the Corner, and he said: Crowd, O son of my brother, for I have seen Abdullah ibn Umar crowd until his nose bled."
"8682","Ibn Jurayj informed us, who said: Ata informed me that he heard Ibn Abbas say: When you find crowding upon the Corner, harm no one nor be harmed, and pass on."
"8683","From al-Thawri, from Jabir, from Abu Abdullah, from Ibn Abbas, who said: I would wish that one who crowds upon the Corner, meaning the Stone, would return even, with neither for him nor against him."
"8684","From al-Thawri and Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu Yafur, from a man: that Umar would crowd upon the Corner, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: O Abu Hafs, you are a strong man, and you harm the weak; so when you find emptiness, touch the Corner, otherwise say tahlil and takbir and pass on."
"8685","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father: that he, when he found crowding upon the Corner, would say takbir and raise his hand and pass on, and would not touch."
"8686","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Muhammad ibn Abbad informed me, from Abu Jafar: that he saw Ibn Abbas come on the Day of Tarwiyah with his head matted. He said: So I saw him kiss the Corner, then prostrate upon it, then kiss it, then prostrate upon it, then kiss it, then prostrate upon it. So I said to Ibn Jurayj: What is matting? He said: It is when a man bathes, then covers his head, so his hair sticks together."
"8687","From Ibn al-Mubarak, or another, from Hanzalah, who said: I heard Tawus say: Umar kissed the Corner, meaning the Stone, then prostrated upon it. Hanzalah said: And I saw Tawus do that."
"8688","From Mamar, from Humayd al-Araj, who said: I heard Mujahid say: The Black Corner does not perish, being from Paradise - meaning: were it not that it is from Paradise, it would have perished."
"8689","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Ata narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Amr and Kab al-Ahbar, that they both said: Were it not for what the impure ones of the ignorance wipe with, no afflicted one would touch it but be healed; and there is nothing of Paradise upon the earth but it."
"8690","From Ibn Jurayj, who said: Mansur ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me that his mother informed him: that the Corner was, before the fire, the color of the Station."
"8691","From al-Aslami, from Salih, freedman of al-Tawamah: that he heard Ibn Abbas say: The Corner is of the stones of Paradise."
"8692","He said: And Husayn informed me, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas: that the Corner and the Station are from Paradise."
"8693","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Salim ibn Abi Hafsah, from Mundhir al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn Ali, who said: And they say: It is of the stones of Paradise; yet it is only a stone from some of these valleys - I think he said: Allah willed to make it a sign."
"8694","From Ibrahim ibn Yazid, that he heard Muhammad ibn Abbad narrating that he heard Ibn Abbas say: The Corner, meaning the Stone, is the right hand of Allah on earth, with which He shakes the hands of His creation as a man shakes the hand of his brother; it bears witness for whoever touches it with piety and fulfillment. By Him in whose Hand is the life of Ibn Abbas, no Muslim servant aligns himself with it asking Allah for good but He gives it to him. Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Muhammad ibn Abbad, from Ibn Abbas, the like of it."
"8695","Ibn Jurayj said: And I was told, from Ali ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Abbas, that he said: The Corner is the right hand of Allah with which He shakes the hands of His servants. Abd al-Razzaq said: So I narrated it to my father, and he said: I heard Wahb ibn Munabbih say: It is the right side of the House; have you not seen that when a man meets his brother he shakes his hand with his right."
"8696","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, who said: Musafi al-Hajabi informed me that he heard a man narrating from Abdullah ibn Umar, that he said: The Corner and the Station are two rubies of the rubies of Paradise; Allah extinguished their light, and were it not for that, they would illuminate what is between the east and the west."
"8697","From al-Aslami, from Muhammad ibn al-Saib, who said: The Corner would be placed upon Abu Qubays and the whole village would shine from its light."
"8698","Ibn Jurayj informed us, who said: I said to Ata: Have you seen people kissing their hands when they touch the Corner - was that of those who passed in everything? He said: Yes; I saw Ibn Umar, and Abu Said al-Khudri, and Jabir ibn Abdullah, and Abu Hurayrah, when they touched, kiss their hands. He said: I said: And Ibn Abbas? He said: And Ibn Abbas, I think. He said: I said: Do you dislike leaving kissing your hand when you touch? He said: Yes; for if he touched, then he would kiss, and I seek its blessing."
"8699","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, who said: He is coarse who touches then does not kiss his hand."
"8700","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) circumambulated the House upon his mount, touching the Corner with his cane, then bringing it to his mouth."
"8701","On the authority of Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed us that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed tawaf on his she-camel. I said: Why? He said: I do not know. Then he dismounted and prayed two rak'ahs for his seven circuits."
"8702","On the authority of Hammad, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arrived while he was ill, he performed tawaf of the House on his mount, touching the Corner with his mihjan (hooked stick), then kissing the tip of the mihjan."
"8703","From Ibn Jurayj and Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed tawaf of the House on his she-camel, touching the Corner with his mihjan. Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf came, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: What did you do, O Abu Muhammad, about touching the Corner? He said: Both; I touched and I left off. He said: You did right."
"8704","From Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed tawaf on a she-camel so that people would not be driven away from him. I said to Hisham: Was it in the Farewell Hajj? He said: Yes, I think so."
"8705","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Murtafi' informed me that he saw Ibn al-Zubayr and Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, when they touched (the Corner), wipe their faces with their hands."
"8706","From Ibn Uyaynah, he said: A shaykh of ours called Humayd ibn Hibban informed me; he said: I saw Salim ibn Abd Allah, when he touched the Corner, place his hand upon his cheek."
"8707","From Ma'mar, he said: I never saw anyone touch (the Corner) except that he kissed his hand, and we found the people upon that. He said: And I often saw Ayyub wipe over his face with his hand when he touched, after kissing his hand."
"8708","From Sa'd ibn Hammad, he said: Musa ibn Abi al-Furat, or someone ibn Abi al-Furat, informed me; he said: I saw Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz touch the Yamani Corner, then kiss his hand, then wipe his face with it."
"8709","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed tawaf of the House on the night of al-Ifadah on his she-camel, touching the Corner with his mihjan."
"8710","From al-Aslami, from Salih, the freedman of al-Taw'amah, that he heard Ibn Abbas say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed tawaf of the House on his mount, out of dislike of blocking the people from him, touching the Corner with his mihjan."
"8711","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: He would be in the mosque, and when he wanted to leave the mosque he would touch the Corner, then leave."
"8712","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to touch the Yamani Corner and the Black Corner, and would not touch the other two."
"8713","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I passed by the mosque without ablution; should I touch the Corner? He said: No. I said: Nor anything of the Ka'bah? He said: No."
"8714","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: One whose right hand is paralyzed and contracted, should he touch with the back of his hand or with his left? He said: Rather he should say takbir and not touch with any of his hands; whichever of those he does is fine. He said: And I had heard him before that say: He should touch with his right even if paralyzed."
"8715","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I passed by the mosque without ablution; should I touch the Corner? He said: No. I said: Nor anything of the Ka'bah? He said: No."
"8716","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, that his father was told the saying of A'ishah: Part of the Hijr is from the House. So Ibn Umar said: By Allah, I think that if A'ishah heard this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then I do not think the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered leaving the touching of the two (corners) except because they are not upon the foundations of Ibrahim, and the people did not perform tawaf behind the Hijr except for that."
"8717","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me that it reached him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not touch the two western corners, but only the two eastern ones."
"8718","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, that he never saw Ibn Umar touch the two western ones; he said: But he would hardly pass the two eastern ones (without touching them)."
"8719","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Ibn Khuthaym, from Abu al-Tufayl, he said: I was with Ibn Abbas and Mu'awiyah while they performed tawaf of the House, and Mu'awiyah would not pass a corner except that he touched it. Ibn Abbas said to him: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would touch only the Yamani Stone. Mu'awiyah said: Nothing of the House is to be left untouched."
"8720","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman ibn Atiq informed me, from Abd Allah ibn Babayh, from one of the sons of Ya'la, from Ya'la ibn Umayyah, he said: I performed tawaf with Umar, and he touched the Corner, and I was on the side toward the House. When we reached the western corner next to the Black one, I pulled his hand so that he would touch it. He said: What is the matter with you? I said: Will you not touch? He said: Did you not perform tawaf with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? I said: Yes. He said: Did you see him touch these two western corners? He said: I said: No. He said: Do you not have in the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a good example? I said: Yes. He said: Then keep away from it."
"8721","From Sa'id ibn al-Sa'ib ibn Yasar, that he heard Ghutayf ibn Abi Sufyan al-Thaqafi narrate that he performed tawaf with Ibn Umar at the House; he said: I saw him never leave the two Yamani corners without touching them in every tawaf. He said: And I saw him not approach the other two."
"8722","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu al-Sha'tha, that he said: And who would avoid anything of the House? He said: And Ibn al-Zubayr used to touch all of them when he began and when he finished."
"8723","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, that his father used to touch all the corners."
"8724","From Ma'mar, he said: I heard Qatadah mention, from a man he named and I forgot him, he said: None of its corners is to be left untouched."
"8725","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ammar al-Duhni, from Abu Sa'id al-Bakri, that al-Hasan and al-Husayn, or one of them, performed tawaf after Asr and touched all the corners."
"8726","From Yasin, from al-Mukhtar, from Sahl ibn Sa'd, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Between the two corners is a basin (?) with seventy thousand saying amen for one who supplicates; and if he forgets, they say: O Allah, forgive him."
"8727","From Ibn al-Mubarak, from Asim ibn Sulayman, that he saw Anas ibn Malik touch all the corners."
"8728","From Ma'mar, from Humayd, from Mujahid, he said: The Maqam was beside the House, and they feared floods overwhelming it, and they performed tawaf behind it. Umar said to al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wada'ah al-Sahmi: Do you know where its first place was? He said: Yes; I measured what is between it and the Black Stone, and between it and the door, and between it and Zamzam, and between it and the corner at the Stone. He said: Then where is its measure? He said: It is with me. He said: Bring its measure. So he brought its measure, and he placed it in its present place."
"8729","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr and Umar, in part of his caliphate, used to pray toward the side of the House, until Umar prayed behind the Maqam."
"8730","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ata and others of our companions claim that Umar was the first who raised the Maqam and placed it in its present place; and it had been facing the Ka'bah."
"8731","From Ibn Jurayj, from Muhammad ibn Abbad ibn Ja'far and Amr ibn Abd Allah ibn Safwan and others, that Umar arrived and lodged in the house of Ibn Siba'. He said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, to Abd Allah ibn al-Sa'ib, and he ordered him to place the Maqam in its present place. He said: And Umar complained of his head, and said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, lead the people in Maghrib. He said: So I prayed ahead of him, and I was the first who prayed ahead of him when it was placed; then he said: Then I sensed Umar, and I had prayed one rak'ah, so he prayed behind me the rest."
"8732","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Have you seen anyone kiss the Maqam or touch it? He said: As for anyone of note, no."
"8733","From al-Thawri, from Nusayr ibn Dhu'luq, that Ibn al-Zubayr saw the people wiping the Maqam and forbade them, saying: You were not commanded to wipe; he said: You were only commanded to pray."
"8734","From al-Thawri, from Mughirah, from his father, he said: I saw al-Hajjaj want to place his foot upon the Maqam, and Ibn al-Hanafiyyah rebuked him for that and forbade him that."
"8735","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Bukayr ibn Abd Allah al-Muzani, he said: I saw Ibn Umar, when he wanted to pray behind the Maqam, place between himself and the Maqam a row or two rows, or one man or two men."
"8736","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: A'ishah said: Allah made tawaf of the House, and (sa'y) between al-Safa and al-Marwah, and the stoning of the pillars only for establishing the remembrance of Allah Almighty. He said: Then a man followed him to hear what he said, and he was saying: Our Lord, give us good in this world and good in the Hereafter and shield us from the punishment of the Fire, until he finished. The man said to him: May Allah keep you well; I followed you and did not hear you add to such-and-such of this saying of yours. He said: Is not that all good? Ata said: Whoever performs tawaf of the House should leave talk and remember Allah, except harmless talk; and I prefer that he leave all talk except remembrance of Allah and the Qur'an."
"8737","Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ata, he said: I performed tawaf behind Ibn Umar and Ibn Abbas, and I did not hear either of them speak during tawaf."
"8738","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Yahya ibn Ubayd, the freedman of al-Sa'ib, informed me that his father told him that Abd Allah ibn al-Sa'ib told him that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say, between the corner of Banu Madhhij and the Black Corner: Our Lord, give us good in this world and good in the Hereafter and shield us from the punishment of the Fire."
"8739","Al-Thawri informed us, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Abu Shu'bah al-Bakri, he said: I watched Ibn Umar while he performed tawaf of the House, saying: There is no god but Allah alone, without partner; His is the dominion and His is the praise; in His hand is good, and He has power over all things. Then he said: Our Lord, give us good in this world and good in the Hereafter and shield us from the punishment of the Fire."
"8740","And I heard a man narrate to Hisham ibn Hassan, from an uncle of his, from Abu Shu'bah al-Bakri, he said: I performed tawaf with Ibn Umar, and I heard him, when he came parallel to the Yamani Corner, say: There is no god but Allah alone, without partner; His is the dominion and His is the praise, and in His hand is good, and He has power over all things. When he came to the Stone, he said: Our Lord, give us good in this world and good in the Hereafter and shield us from the punishment of the Fire. When he finished, I said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, I heard you say such-and-such. He said: Did you hear me? I said: Yes. He said: Then it is that: I praised my Lord, bore a true testimony, and asked Him for the good of this world and the Hereafter. So Hisham called for an inkpot and wrote it."
"8741","From Ma'mar, he said: One whom I trust informed me, from a man, he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab murmuring around the House, saying: Our Lord, give us good in this world and good in the Hereafter and shield us from the punishment of the Fire."
"8742","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Whoever performs tawaf of the House should leave talk and remember Allah, except harmless talk; and I prefer that he leave all talk except remembrance of Allah and the Qur'an; from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Ata."
"8743","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Ata, he said: They used to perform tawaf and talk. He said: And Ata was asked about reciting (Qur'an) during tawaf, and he said: It is an innovation."
"8744","From Ibrahim ibn Yazid, he said: al-Walid ibn Abd Allah informed me; he said: We used to recite the Qur'an to Mujahid while he performed tawaf of the House."
"8745","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Abi Najih: He was asked about reciting during tawaf, and he said: The people innovated it."
"8746","From al-Aslami ibn Abi Bakrah, from Yahya al-Bakka', that he heard Ibn Umar dislike reciting during tawaf, saying: It is an innovation."
"8747","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: al-Hasan ibn Muslim informed me, from Tawus, from a man who had met the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Tawaf is but a prayer; so when you perform tawaf, speak little."
"8748","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Tawaf is a prayer; so when you perform tawaf, speak little."
"8749","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibrahim ibn Maysarah informed me, from Tawus, that he said: Ibn Abbas said: When you perform tawaf, speak little, for it is but a prayer."
"8750","From Ja'far ibn Sulayman, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Tawus or Ikrimah, or both, that Ibn Abbas said: Tawaf is a prayer, but speech has been permitted to you in it; so whoever speaks should speak only good."
"8751","From Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad, I heard him say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) met a man during tawaf and said: How are you this morning? How do you feel? How many are with you?"
"8752","From Yahya ibn al-Ala, from Talhah, from Ata, he said: While Umar ibn al-Khattab was performing tawaf of the Ka'bah, he heard two men behind him speaking gibberish (non-Arabic), so he turned to them and said to them: Find your way to Arabic."
"8753","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, he said: I was performing tawaf with Tawus, and he said: Touch (the Corner) for us; this is our fifth. He said: So I thought he liked to touch on an odd number."
"8754","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Kathir ibn Kathir informed me that he performed tawaf with Sa'id ibn Jubayr, and the prayer cut them off, while two tawafs remained for them; so Sa'id did not repeat them and left upon five circuits."
"8755","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Sulayman al-Ahwal informed me, from one who performed tawaf with Abu al-Sha'tha: The prayer interrupted him while part of his tawaf remained, so he did not repeat what remained; and I thought he left upon five circuits."
"8756","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: The prayer interrupted me; should I complete what remains? He said: Yes. A man said to him: But I turned back? He said: Then build upon what has passed. I said: The prayer interrupted me and I prayed at the Maqam, or near the house of Ibn al-Zubayr, or on your side? He said: Leave that tawaf and do not count it. I said: What if I prayed on your side; should I not proceed when I finish as I am toward the Corner and count it as nothing? He said: Yes, if you wish. Until after that, I said: The tawaf which the prayer interrupts while I am in it? He said: I prefer that you not count it. I said: But if I counted it, would it suffice? He said: Yes, Allah willing; you have performed tawaf. And Amr ibn Dinar says it."
"8757","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: How should one do? He said: If you see him (the imam) come out while you are at the Corner and you have not performed tawaf. I said: Then he comes out while you have left the Corner behind? He said: If you think you will complete that tawaf, go on and perform tawaf; otherwise shorten it. I said: The prayer interrupted my seven circuits, so I left and wanted to bow (pray) before completing my seven? He said: No; complete your seven, unless (congregational) tawaf prevents you, then pray if you wish, until you leave."
"8758","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: How long should I sit after the imam's salutation if I was interrupted? He said: Nothing; and do not sit for talk. I said: Should I break my tawaf for a funeral to pray over it, then return? He said: No. Amr ibn Dinar says it."
"8759","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: If the prayer interrupts your tawaf, complete what remains upon what has passed, and do not bow (pray the two rak'ahs) if the prayer interrupts your tawaf until you complete it."
"8760","From Hisham ibn Hassan, from Ata, concerning a man who performed some circuits, then the prayer was established or the prayer caught him, so he left: He said: If his tawaf was voluntary, then if it was odd it suffices him, whether he prayed two rak'ahs or not; and if he wishes he may complete his tawaf, whether even or odd, then pray. And it pleased him that he not leave except upon an odd number of that seven."
"8761","From Hisham, from a companion of his: Concerning one who performed tawaf with Sa'id ibn Jubayr for five circuits, then the Asr prayer was established: He completed the rest of his tawaf, then prayed the two rak'ahs of tawaf after Asr."
"8762","From al-Aslami, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Whoever performs tawaf of the House and a need appears to him should leave upon an odd number and pray two rak'ahs, and should not return for the rest of the seven."
"8763","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that he said: If the prayer interrupts your seven, complete it from where you stopped."
"8764","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said: May a man rest and sit during tawaf? He said: Yes. He said: And Ata disliked one saying: rounds; say: tawaf."
"8765","From al-Thawri, he said: Jamil ibn Zayd informed me that he saw Ibn Umar perform tawaf on a hot day for three tawafs, then sit in the Hijr and rest, then stand and complete upon what had passed."
"8766","Ibn Abi Rawwad informed us, from Nafi', he said: I never saw Ibn Umar standing during tawaf at all except when touching the Corner."
"8767","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I saw Ibn al-Zubayr perform tawaf of the House walking quickly."
"8768","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told that Nafi' said: I never saw Ibn Umar standing during tawaf. He said: And it is said that standing during tawaf is an innovation."
"8769","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: If a man performs part of his seven inside the Hijr, let him perform tawaf of the House from behind the Hijr for what he performed inside the Hijr, if he missed it."
"8770","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Hisham ibn Hujayr, from Tawus or another, from Ibn Abbas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed tawaf from behind the Hijr. Ibn Uyaynah said: And my father informed me that he saw Hisham ibn Abd al-Malik performing tawaf from behind it, and he wanted to enter the Hijr and perform tawaf inside it, so Salim ibn Abd Allah pulled him until he performed tawaf from behind it."
"8771","From his father, he said: I heard Marthad ibn Shurahbil say: I heard Ibn Abbas say: If I were in charge of anything of the House, I would include the whole Hijr in it, so that tawaf would not be performed behind it."
"8772","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: It reached me that the obligatory prayer suffices for the two rak'ahs for the seven."
"8773","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Abu al-Sha'tha said: The obligatory (prayer) suffices for the two rak'ahs of the seven; from Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, the same."
"8774","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: I performed tawaf with Mujahid, seven circuits after Asr, then we sat waiting for Maghrib prayer, and he prayed. I said: Will you not bow for your tawaf? He said: The obligatory suffices us."
"8775","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told, from Muslim ibn Murrah al-Jumahi, that he performed tawaf with Ibn Umar before sunset; he said: We finished and the prayer was established, so we prayed Maghrib. Then he stood and did not pray, and began another seven. I said: You did not pray for your seven. He said: Did we not pray? Then he said: The obligatory prayer suffices for the two rak'ahs of the seven."
"8776","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Yahya ibn Qamtah, he said: I asked Salim ibn Abd Allah; I said: I finished tawaf and the prayer was established? He said: The prayer suffices you for your tawaf."
"8777","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said: The two rak'ahs of Fajr suffice for the two rak'ahs for the seven."
"8778","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: It was said to him: The obligatory prayer suffices for the two rak'ahs for the seven. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never performed seven circuits except that he prayed two rak'ahs for it."
"8779","From Hisham, from al-Hasan, that he performed tawaf of the House, then prayed the obligatory, then prayed the two rak'ahs of tawaf."
"8780","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, he said: I asked Sa'id ibn Jubayr about tawaf after Asr; he said: He said: If you wish, bow when the sun has set; and if you wish, the obligatory suffices you; and if you wish, bow them after the obligatory."
"8781","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I said to Ata: Would my seven suffice if I do not pray until I come to the House and pray them? He said: Yes, if you wish. I said: What if I offered the two rak'ahs of the seven before it; would that suffice for the two after it? He said: Glory be to Allah, I do not know. He said: I said: No, until I bow them after it? He said: Yes."
"8782","From al-Thawri, he said: Bow them wherever you wish, so long as you do not leave the Sanctuary."
"8783","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, he said: His father used to perform tawaf of the House and see it open, so he would enter and pray, then come out and pray the two rak'ahs of tawaf outside the House."
"8784","From al-Thawri, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Salim ibn Abd Allah, from Ibn Umar, that he used to perform tawaf of the House seven times, then enter the House and pray the two rak'ahs of tawaf in it; from Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, the same."
"8785","Abd al-Wahhab informed us, he said: Mandal narrated to us; he said: Layth narrated to us, that Tawus and Ibn Sabit used to pray four rak'ahs for every week (seven circuits)."
"8786","Mandal said: So I told it to Ibn Jurayj, and he said: Ata narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray two rak'ahs for every seven."
"8787","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Banu Abd al-Muttalib: O sons of Abd Manaf, if any of the matter comes to you, let me not know that any of you prevents any of the people from performing tawaf of the House or praying at it at any hour of night or day. He said: Then Abd al-Malik came as a pilgrim and forbade tawaf after dawn for a day or two, then permitted it at that time; and it was narrated to me that this hadith reached him."
"8788","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Abd Allah ibn Babayh report, from Jubayr ibn Mut'im, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the report of Ata: O sons of Abd al-Muttalib, O sons of Abd Manaf: Let me not know that you prevent any of the people from praying at this House at any hour he wishes of night or day."
"8789","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ibn Abi Awfa mention that he saw Ibn Abbas on the day of al-Tarwiyah perform tawaf after Asr, seven circuits, then pray two rak'ahs, as pilgrim and umrah-performer; and he would stand after the dawn prayer and perform tawaf, seven circuits, and bow two rak'ahs. We said to him: He only does that because of his arrival, until he stayed among us; then he stood when he prayed dawn, performed tawaf, then bowed two rak'ahs, then touched the Corner and went up - meaning he left the mosque. Ata said: And I saw Ibn al-Zubayr perform tawaf after dawn, seven circuits, and pray two rak'ahs, then mount."
"8790","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he used to perform tawaf after Asr and after dawn, and pray for his seven at that time."
"8791","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Salim ibn Abd Allah, he said: Ibn Umar saw no harm in tawaf after Asr, and would pray two rak'ahs at that time."
"8792","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, that Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Qari informed him that he performed tawaf with Umar after the dawn prayer at the Ka'bah; when Umar finished his tawaf he looked and did not see the sun, so he mounted and did not glorify (pray) until he stopped at Dhu Tuwa, where he glorified two rak'ahs for his tawaf."
"8793","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, he said: I saw Sa'id ibn Jubayr and Mujahid perform tawaf after Asr, one seven, then sit and not pray until the sun set."
"8794","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, from his father, he said: Abu Sa'id al-Khudri arrived as pilgrim or umrah-performer, and performed tawaf after dawn. He said: Watch what he does. When he finished his seven he sat; when the sun rose he prayed two rak'ahs."
"8795","From al-Aslami, from Musa ibn Uqbah, he said: I asked Ata ibn Abi Rabah about tawaf after Asr and after dawn, and he said: I saw Ibn Umar perform tawaf after dawn, then pray. Musa said: So I went to Nafi' and told him, and he said: Ata lied. So I returned to Ata and told him, and he said: I saw Ibn Umar do that before Nafi' was taken captive. Musa said: So I went to Salim ibn Abd Allah and asked him, and he said: Ata spoke the truth; Ibn Umar used to perform tawaf after dawn, one seven, then pray for it at that time. Musa said: So I went to Nafi' and mentioned to him the saying of Salim, and he fell silent."
"8796","Ma'mar informed us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar used to dislike combining tawafs, saying: For every seven there are two rak'ahs. And he himself would not combine two sevens."
"8797","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus: That his father saw no harm in combining tawaf, and would sometimes do it."
"8798","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ata saw no harm in combining tawaf, and would give fatwa for it; and he mentions that Tawus and al-Miswar ibn Makhramah used to do it. He said: And a man asked Ata about performing several sevens with no bowing between them, until he bows their bowings after finishing them. He said: That reached me from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and from Tawus, and I think it is only something that reached them. I said to Ata: Did that reach you from anyone besides them? He said: He said: And what is wrong if I did it? He said: I think there is no harm in it if you did it."
"8799","Ibn Jurayj said: And Amr ibn Dinar said: It reached me, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, that he used to perform several sevens without bowing between them."
"8800","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Abd al-Karim informed me; he said: I performed tawaf with Sa'id ibn Jubayr on the day of Fitr before the Fitr prayer, and he combined three sevens. I said: What is the matter with you combining? He said: One does not pray before the Fitr prayer."
"8801","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told that A'ishah lodged in the dwelling of Utbah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Harith, and she used to perform tawaf after the last Isha; when she wanted to perform tawaf she ordered the lamps of the mosque to be extinguished all together, then she performed tawaf. When she finished a seven she sought refuge (in supplication) between the Corner and the door, then returned to the Corner, touched it, and performed another seven. When she finished, she sought refuge from it between the Corner and the door, then returned and combined three weeks (sevens). Then she went behind the bench of Zamzam, then prayed two rak'ahs, then spoke, then prayed two rak'ahs, separating between every two rak'ahs with speech. With her were a freedwoman, and Umm Hakim daughter of Khalid ibn al-As, and Umm Hakim daughter of Abd Allah ibn Abi Rabi'ah. The freedwoman said: We mentioned Hassan and discussed his lineage, so A'ishah said: Son of al-Furay'ah pleases him (?); and she forbade us to revile him, and cleared him of being among those who slandered her, saying: I hope Allah will admit him to Paradise for his saying: You lampooned Muhammad and I answered for him, and with Allah is the reward in that; for my father and his father and my honor are a shield for the honor of Muhammad against you. And A'ishah would recite these two verses while performing tawaf of the House."
"8802","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Muhammad ibn al-Sa'ib ibn Barakah al-Makki, from his mother, that she performed tawaf with A'ishah at the House, three sevens without praying between them; when she finished she prayed two rak'ahs for every seven."
"8803","Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: Ata informed me that Ibn Hisham forbade women to perform tawaf with men. So he told me, saying: How would you forbid them tawaf? The wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed tawaf with men. I said: After the veil? He said: Yes, by my life; I indeed witnessed after the veil. I said: How did they mix with men? He said: They did not; A'ishah used to perform tawaf apart from men, not mixing with them. A woman with her said: Come with us, O Mother of the Believers, let us touch. She pulled her and said: Go away from yourself (?), and refused to touch. They used to come out veiled at night and perform tawaf with men without mixing with them. He said: But when they entered the House they were screened when they entered, then the men were taken out from them. He said: And I used to visit A'ishah, I and Ubayd ibn Umayr, while she was staying in the interior of Thabir. I said: Then what was her veil at that time? He said: She was in a small Turkish tent of hers, with a cover over it, between us and her. He said: And I saw upon her a saffron-dyed shift when I was a boy."
"8804","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata also informed me; he said: It reached me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Umm Salamah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), to perform tawaf riding in her litter, behind those praying in the interior of the mosque. I said: By day or night? He said: I do not know. I said: Which seven? He said: I do not know."
"8805","From Ma'mar, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah informed me; he said: Sawdah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), went out one night, and Umar saw her - and she was tall - and said: You will not be hidden from us. That was mentioned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was eating a bone with meat, and he did not put it down before it was revealed to him: That you have been permitted to go out at night for your needs."
"8806","Malik informed us, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Nawfal, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Zaynab bint Abi Salamah, from Umm Salamah, she said: I sent to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), or I complained to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that I was ill. He said: Perform tawaf behind the people while riding. She said: I performed tawaf while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with the people beside the House, reciting: By al-Tur and a Book inscribed. Abd al-Razzaq said: Hajizah means secluded, separated between them and men by a cloth. He said: And al-Turkiyyah is a small tent of felt pitched on the ground."
"8807","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is it disliked for a man to perform tawaf before the prayer while the imam's coming out is awaited? He said: It does him no harm. I said: Then in the yellowness of the sun, at the time prayer is disliked, if he delays his two rak'ahs until it is a time prayer is not disliked? He said: And what harms him, if he does not pray at the time prayer is disliked?"
"8808","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Layth, that Tawus, Mujahid and Ata forbade him to perform tawaf beyond the Maqam, saying: What is between the House and the Maqam."
"8809","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: A boy who has not reached puberty, if he is carried in tawaf of the House, should he perform ablution? He said: It is nothing upon him; there is nothing upon one who understands but to seek blessing in his ablution. Sufyan said: That seven suffices for both of them together."
"8810","From al-Thawri, from Abu Bakr bi-Haqq, that Abu Bakr carried Ibn al-Zubayr in tawaf in a cloth."
"8811","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I used to hear strangers ask Ata: Is tawaf better for us or prayer? And he would say: As for you, tawaf is better; you cannot perform tawaf in your lands, while you can pray there."
"8812","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told, from Anas ibn Malik, that he arrived in Madinah, and Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz wrote to him asking: Is prayer better for strangers or tawaf? Anas said to him: Rather prayer and enjoyment of the House is better."
"8813","From al-Thawri, from Salim, he said: I saw Sa'id ibn Jubayr say to strangers when he saw them praying: Go and perform tawaf of the House."
"8814","From Fudayl, from Hisham, from al-Hasan and Ata, they said: When a stranger stays in Makkah forty days, prayer is better for him than tawaf."
"8815","From Malik, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Mulaykah, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah have mercy on him, passed by a leprous woman performing tawaf of the House, and said to her: O bondwoman of Allah, do not harm people; if you sat in your house. So she did. Then a man passed by her after that and said: The one who forbade you has died, so come out. She said: I would not obey him alive and disobey him dead."
"8816","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Kissing the Corner? He said: Good."
"8817","From Ma'mar, from Asim, from Abd Allah ibn Sarjis, he said: I saw Umar ibn al-Khattab kiss the Corner, saying: By Allah, I kiss you while I know you are a stone, and I know that Allah is my Lord; but I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kiss you, so I kiss you."
"8818","From Isra'il, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abd al-A'la informed me, from Suwayd ibn Ghaflah, he said: I saw Umar ibn al-Khattab kiss the Stone, saying: By Allah, I know you are a stone, but I saw Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) devoted to you."
"8819","From Muhammad ibn Rashid, he said: I heard Makhul narrate that Umar ibn al-Khattab faced the Corner and said: I know you are a stone, and you neither harm nor benefit; and were it not that I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kiss you, I would not kiss you. He said: Then he kissed it."
"8820","Al-Thawri informed us, and more than one, from al-Hasan ibn Abd Allah, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas: Then he wiped the Corner with his garment, then kissed it."
"8821","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me; he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not seek refuge (at the Multazam). He said: And he told me that he never saw Abu Hurayrah, nor Jabir, nor Abu Sa'id, nor Ibn Umar cling to any part of the House at Zamzam's side. I said: Did it reach you that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to touch anything of its interior or its steps seeking refuge by it? He said: No. I said: Nor from any of his companions? He said: No. I said: And did you never see any of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do that? He said: No. I said: Do you yourself cling to the House? He said: No; but I place my hand toward the House without touching it at all. I said: Then outside the House do you cling to it? He said: No. He said: And why would I seek refuge by any part of it; I would not care which part I sought refuge by; I do not follow anything in that case."
"8822","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata informed me, from Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan, that he sought refuge at the House, and al-Harith ibn Abd Allah said to him: Do you know, O Commander of the Believers, who first did this? He said: No. He said: The old women of your people, the old women of Quraysh. He said: So I thought Abd al-Malik left that afterwards."
"8823","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to place his hand upon the Yamani Corner."
"8824","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus: That his father used to seek refuge between the Corner and the door."
"8825","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid: And it is disliked for a man to place his forehead upon the House, but (only) his hand."
"8826","From Ma'mar, he said: I saw Ayyub press his chest and his hands against the House."
"8827","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: I performed tawaf with Abd Allah ibn Amr; when we finished the seven we bowed (prayed) behind the Ka'bah. I said: Will you not seek refuge? He said: I seek refuge in Allah from the Fire. Then he walked, touched the Corner, then stood between the Stone and the door, pressed his chest, hands and cheek to it, then said: Thus I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do."
"8828","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Shu'ayb said: Muhammad, his grandfather, performed tawaf with his father Abd Allah ibn Amr; when it was their seven, Muhammad said to Abd Allah where they seek refuge: Seek refuge. Abd Allah said: I seek refuge in Allah from Satan. When he touched the Corner, he sought refuge between the Corner and the door, pressed his forehead and chest to the House, then said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do this."
"8829","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Humayd al-A'raj informed me, from Mujahid, he said: I came to Ibn Abbas while he sought refuge between the Corner and the door, leaning upon the hand of Ikrimah his freedman. I said: Two magicians supporting each other, or two magics? - so he would not return them. Ikrimah said: Two magicians supporting each other; I pressed him much."
"8830","From Sa'id ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Makhul: I said: When you perform tawaf, between the sixth and the seventh? I said: Then I cling to the House between the Black Corner and the Yamani Corner, then I seek refuge in Allah."
"8831","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Mujahid, he said: Ibn Abbas said: This Multazam is between the Corner and the door."
"8832","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, that he used to press his chest, hand and belly against the House."
"8833","From Abd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that he would not cling to anything of the House."
"8834","And as for Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told, from Ibn Umar, that he used to seek refuge between the Corner and the door."
"8835","He said: Ma'mar informed us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', that Ibn Umar would not cling to anything of the House."
"8836","From Ibn Umar, he said: I heard Uthman ibn al-Aswad say: I saw Mujahid pass by a man standing supplicating between the Corner and the door, and he touched him with his hand, saying: Cling, cling."
"8837","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Did it reach you that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) or any of his companions used to face the House when leaving and supplicate? He said: No."
"8838","Then Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn al-As informed me that he said to some who face the House: Thus one supplicates when leaving, at his leaving: Why do they do? This is the practice of the Jews in their book. Supplicate in the House whatever occurs to you, then leave."
"8839","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he came parallel to a door in the house of Ya'la, at the grain-sellers, would face the House and supplicate; and the daughters of Ghazwan came out to him - and they were Muslims - and would supplicate with him."
"8840","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Yazid informed us that Abd al-Rahman ibn Tariq ibn Alqamah told him, from his mother, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he came parallel to a place of the house of Ya'la - Ubayd Allah forgot it - would face the House, then supplicate. Ibn Jurayj said: And I myself would perform tawaf with Abd Allah ibn Kathir al-Dari until, when we came to that place, he would face the House, then supplicate, saying: Something has reached me about this place."
"8841","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: I heard Ibn Abbas say: You were only commanded tawaf, and were not commanded to enter it. He said: He would not forbid entering it."
"8842","But I heard him say: Usamah ibn Zayd informed me, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): When he entered the House he supplicated in all its sides and did not pray in it until he came out; when he came out he bowed two rak'ahs facing the qiblah, saying: This is the qiblah. I said: What are its sides? In its corners? He said: Rather in every direction of the House. And I think I saw al-Hasan ibn Ali enter the House and supplicate in all its sides, and not pray in it; then he came out and bowed two rak'ahs toward the qiblah."
"8843","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Ibn Abbas: Al-Fadl ibn Abbas would tell him that he entered the House with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not pray in the House when he entered it, but when he came out he alighted and bowed two rak'ahs at the door of the House."
"8844","From Muhammad, from Uthman al-Jazari, that he heard Miqsam narrate from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the House and supplicated in its sides, then came out and prayed two rak'ahs."
"8845","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Mis'ar, from Simak al-Hanafi, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas say: Follow it all, and do not leave any part of it behind you."
"8846","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he would not pray inside the House, and when he came out he would pray two rak'as."
"8847","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the House, then came out, and he did not mention that he prayed in it."
"8848","Ibn Jurayj reported, from Ata, that he saw Ibn Umar praying in it. Ata said: and I pray in it."
"8849","He said: and Amr ibn Dinar told me, from one of the gatekeepers, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in the House. I said to Ata: where did it reach you that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in the House? Mark it for me as you remember. He said: there were six pillars in the House. He said: it reached me that he prayed between the two pillars, where he placed the ring. I said: would you pray in it facing every direction? He said: yes. Abd al-Razzaq said: and I pray in it."
"8850","From Ibn Jurayj, Amr told me, from Ibn Umar, he reported from Bilal: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in it two rak'as."
"8851","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came on the day of the conquest of Makkah upon the she-camel of Usama ibn Zayd until he made it kneel at the courtyard of the Ka'ba, then he called for Uthman with the key, then he went to his mother and she refused to give it to him. He said: by Allah, you shall surely give it, or this sword shall surely come forth from my loins. When she saw that, she gave it to him, and he brought it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he opened the door. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered, with Bilal and Usama. He said: and I think he said: Uthman ibn Talha. He said: and I think he said: al-Fadl ibn al-Abbas. They remained shut in with them a while. He said: I was a young strong man, so I hastened ahead of the people and outran them, and I found Bilal standing at the door. I said: O Bilal, where did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray? He said: between the two front columns he prayed two rak'as. Other than Ma'mar reported from Ayyub that he said: I forgot to ask how many he prayed."
"8852","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ibn Abi Mulayka and others narrating this hadith, some adding to others. He said: Abdullah ibn Umar said: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came on the day of the conquest upon the camel of Usama ibn Zayd, with Usama riding behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and with him Bilal and Uthman ibn Talha. When he came to the House he sent Uthman ibn Talha and he brought the key to him, and he opened it. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered, with Usama ibn Zayd, Uthman ibn Talha and Bilal. They remained in the House a long time, and they locked the door. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to them, and they raced to the House. Abdullah ibn Umar outran them, he and another with him. Abdullah asked them, asking Bilal: where did the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray? He showed him where he prayed, and he did not ask him how many he prayed. He said: when Abdullah ibn Umar entered the Ka'ba he would walk toward his face with the door behind his back, then walk until between him and the wall was nearly three cubits, then pray, aiming for the place Bilal had told him the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in."
"8853","From Ibn Uyayna, from Mis'ar, from Simak al-Hanafi, he said: I heard Ibn Umar say: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in the House, or in the Ka'ba, and another will come forbidding you, so do not obey him — meaning Ibn Abbas."
"8854","From Ibn Uyayna, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah, he said: I heard Abu Hafs say: the statement of Ibn Umar is dearer to me than the statement of Ibn Abbas."
"8855","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: one of the gatekeepers told me, that Nafi told him, that Ibn Umar told him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed between the two Yemeni pillars."
"8856","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar would circumambulate the House seven times, then enter the House and pray in it the two rak'as of tawaf."
"8857","From al-Thawri, he said: Yazid told me, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, that Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyya entered the Ka'ba and prayed two rak'as in each corner. Al-Thawri said: and Muhammad ibn Ja'far told me, from his father, that al-Husayn ibn Ali entered the Ka'ba and prayed two rak'as."
"8858","From Isra'il, he said: Ash'ath ibn Abi al-Sha'tha told me, from his father, he said: I heard Ibn Umar say: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came walking between Usama ibn Zayd and Bilal until he entered the Ka'ba, and in it was a crossbeam. When Bilal came out I asked him: how did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do? He said: he left a third of the beam on his right and prayed in the remaining two-thirds. He said: I said: how many did he pray? He said: I did not ask Bilal about that."
"8859","Al-Thawri reported, from Layth, from Ata, Tawus and Mujahid, they said: the House is not entered with shoes, nor weapons, nor leather socks. Ata and Mujahid held the Hijr to be part of the House."
"8860","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Uthman ibn Talha on the day of the conquest: bring me the key of the Ka'ba. He was slow with him, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood waiting for him, until sweat like pearls dripped from him, and he said: what detains him? A man hastened to him, and the woman who had the key — I think he said: she was the mother of Uthman — said: if he takes it from you, he will never return it to you. He kept at her until she gave him the key, and he brought it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) opened the House, then came out, with the people with him, and he sat near the siqaya. Ali said: if we were given prophethood, and given the siqaya, and given the hijaba, what people would have a greater share than us? He said: it was as if the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked his remark. Then he called Uthman ibn Talha and handed him the key, and said: conceal it. I narrated it to Ibn Uyayna."
"8861","He said: Ibn Jurayj told me, from Ibn Abi Mulayka, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Ali that day when he spoke to him about the key: I have only given you what you bear expense in, and I have not given you what you take from — meaning: I gave you the siqaya because you spend upon it, and I did not give you the House, meaning that by taking it they take from its gifts. The saying of Abd al-Razzaq."
"8862","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab said: when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) handed the key to Uthman, he said: conceal it."
"8863","From al-Aslami, he said: Muhammad ibn Mu'aqqib narrated to me, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took the key of the Ka'ba on the day of the conquest, with the people present. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: will anyone speak? Then he called Talha, then he called Uthman ibn Talha, and handed him the key."
"8864","From one of our companions, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Abi Mulayka narrated to me, he said: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Uthman ibn Talha on the day of the conquest with the key of the Ka'ba. He came with it uncovered until he handed it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Al-Abbas said: O Prophet of Allah, join the hijaba for me with the siqaya. Revelation came down upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: call Uthman ibn Talha for me. He was called for him, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) handed it to him and kept him under cover. He said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was the first who kept him under cover. Then he said: take it, O sons of Talha; none shall take it from you except a wrongdoer."
"8865","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, that he disliked prayer upon the roof of the Ka'ba."
"8866","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: does one pray upon the roof of the Ka'ba, one who climbs upon it? He said: I do not like that. I said: what if the gatekeepers are overtaken by prayer while upon it, do you dislike that they pray upon it then? He said: yes, I dislike it."
"8867","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, that some people asked Mu'awiya about a place with no qibla. He asked Ibn Abbas, and he said: the roof of the Ka'ba."
"8868","From Ibn Mujahid, from his father, he said: Heraclius wrote to Mu'awiya asking him about three things: what place, when you pray in it, do you think you have not prayed toward the qibla; what place did the sun rise in once, never before nor after; and about the effacement in the moon? Mu'awiya sought knowledge of that, and he wished to know it without Ibn Abbas, but he did not find it. So he wrote to Ibn Abbas about it, and he wrote to them: as for the place in which, when you pray, you think you have not prayed toward the qibla, it is the roof of the Ka'ba. As for the place in which the sun rose once, never before nor after, it is the sea when Allah parted it for Musa. As for the effacement in the moon, Allah the Exalted says: We effaced the sign of the night — that is the effacement."
"8869","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: one of the gatekeepers told me, he said: Shayba ibn Uthman stripped the Ka'ba, before the fire, of garments the people of Jahiliyya had clothed it with. He perfumed it with khaluq and scented it. He said: he left in it the two horns of the ram, in its exterior structure near the direction of the Maqam. I said: what were those garments? He said: of every kind, coarse cloths and better than that."
"8870","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Shayba ibn Uthman told me, and I asked him: were there two horns of a ram in the House? He said: yes, they were in it. I said: did you see them? He said: I think so, but Abd al-Rahman ibn Babayh told me that he had seen them. He said: and others have seen them in it. He said: and they say they are the two horns of the ram Ibrahim slaughtered. Ibn Jurayj said: Safiyya bint Shayba said: the two horns of the ram were in it. It was narrated to me that Ibn Abbas said: they were in it. He said: and it was narrated to me from an old woman, she said: I saw them in it, upon them the red dye of tanned leather."
"8871","From Ibn Uyayna, from Mansur ibn Safiyya, from his maternal uncle, from his mother, from a woman of Banu Sulaym, she said: I asked Uthman: why did the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) send for you after coming out of the Ka'ba? He said: he sent to me and said: I saw the two horns of the ram, and I did not order you to cover them up, for nothing should be in the House to distract one praying."
"8872","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr, from al-Hasan, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said: if we took what is in this House — meaning the Ka'ba — and divided it... Ubayy ibn Ka'b said to him: by Allah, that is not yours. He said: why? He said: because Allah has explained the place of every wealth, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) confirmed it. He said: you spoke the truth."
"8873","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told that Umar ibn al-Khattab used to clothe it with qibati linen."
"8874","He said: and more than one told me: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) clothed it with qibati and habara cloths, and Abu Bakr, Umar and Uthman did, and the first who clothed it with brocade was Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan. Those of the jurists who saw it said: he did well; we know no clothing of it more befitting than it."
"8875","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: it reached us that Tubba' was the first who clothed the Ka'ba with wasa'il cloths, and it was covered with them. Ibn Jurayj said: one of our scholars claimed it was Isma'il the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Allah knows best about that."
"8876","From al-Aslami, he said: Hisham ibn Urwa told me that Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr was the first who clothed the Ka'ba with brocade."
"8877","From Ma'mar, from Alqama ibn Abi Alqama, from his mother, she said: I asked A'isha: shall we clothe the Ka'ba? She said: the rulers suffice you for that, but purify it yourselves with perfume."
"8878","From Ma'mar, from al-A'mash, from al-Minhal ibn Amr, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas about His saying: and His Throne was upon the water. I said: upon what was the water before anything was created? He said: upon the back of the wind. Ibn Jurayj said: Sa'id ibn Jubayr said: Ibn Abbas said: vapor would rise to the sky like the vapor of rivers, then it hardened and became rock. That is His saying: then He rose to the heaven while it was smoke. Ibn Jurayj said: Amr and Ata said: Allah sent winds, and they struck the water, exposing at the site of the House a dry patch like a dome. This House is of it; hence it is the Mother of Towns. Ibn Jurayj said: Ata said: then Allah pegged it with the mountains lest it overturn. The first mountain was Abu Qubays."
"8879","From Hisham ibn Hassan, he said: Sawwar narrated to me, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, he said: when Allah sent Adam down, his feet were upon the earth and his head in the heaven, hearing the speech of the people of heaven and their supplication, and he felt comfort with them. The angels were awed by him until they complained to Allah in their supplication in their prayer. So Allah lowered him to the earth. When he missed what he used to hear from them, he felt lonely, until he complained to Allah in his supplication and in his prayer. So He directed him to Makkah. Each place of his foot became a village, and his stride a desert, until he reached Makkah. Allah sent down a ruby of the rubies of Paradise, and it was upon the site of the House now. Circumambulation of it continued until Allah sent the Flood, and that ruby was raised. Then Allah sent Ibrahim, and he built it. That is the saying of Allah, Exalted: when We assigned to Ibrahim the site of the House."
"8880","From Umar ibn Hawshab, he said: I heard Amr ibn Dinar mention that the House was raised on the day of the drowning."
"8881","From Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Adam said: O Lord, why do I not hear the voices of the angels? He said: your sin. But descend to the earth, build for Me a house, then circle it as you saw the angels circle My house in the heaven. It is claimed he built it from five mountains: Hira, Lebanon, al-Judi, Mount Zita and Mount Sinai, and its foundation was from Hira. This was the building of Adam; then Ibrahim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) built it. He mentioned it from Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn al-Musayyib and others."
"8882","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, he said: the Ka'ba was built from five mountains: Lebanon, Mount Zita, al-Judi, Mount Sinai and Hira, and its foundation was from Hira."
"8883","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: some people said: Allah sent therein a head, and the head said: O Ibrahim, your Lord commands you to take the extent of this cloud. He kept looking at it and marking its extent. The head said: have you done? He said: yes. It rose, and he dug, exposing a firm foundation in the earth."
"8884","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Mujahid said: the angel, the shrike and the Sakina came with Ibrahim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from Sham. The Sakina said: O Ibrahim, settle upon the House. He said: hence no rude Bedouin circumambulates the House, nor any king among kings, but you see dignity and tranquility upon him."
"8885","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: Allah placed the House with Adam. Allah sent Adam down to the earth, and his landing was in the land of India. His head was in the heaven and his feet upon the earth, and the angels were awed by him. So he was reduced to sixty cubits. Adam grieved when he missed the voices of the angels and their glorification, and he complained of that to Allah the Exalted. Allah said: O Adam, I have sent down for you a house; circumambulate it as is circumambulated around My Throne, and pray at it as is prayed at My Throne. So Adam went out to it, and his stride was extended for him, with a desert between each stride, and those deserts remained after that. Adam came to the House and circumambulated it, and the prophets after him. Ma'mar said: Aban told me the House was sent down as a single ruby or a single pearl. Ma'mar said: it reached me that the ark of Nuh circumambulated the House seven times. When Allah drowned the people of Nuh, He raised it, and its foundation remained. So He assigned it to Ibrahim, and he built it after that. That is the saying of Allah: when We assigned to Ibrahim."
"8886","From Hisham ibn Hassan, he said: Humayd al-A'raj narrated to me, from Mujahid, he said: Allah created the site of this House two thousand years before creating anything of the earth, and its pillars are in the seventh earth."
"8887","From Ibn Uyayna, he said: Ka'b narrated to me that the House was foam upon the water forty years before the earth was created, and from it the earth was spread. He said: and Ibn Abi Talib narrated to us: that Ibrahim came from Armenia with him, and he guided him until he settled the House as the spider settles its web. He said: they uncovered stones, a stone a man could bear — or he said: thirty men could not bear. He said: I said: O Abu Muhammad, Allah says: when Ibrahim raised the foundations of the House. He said: that was after."
"8888","From Ibn Jurayj, Ibn al-Musayyib said: Ibn Abi Talib said: Allah had entrusted the Corner to Abu Qubays. When Ibrahim came, Abu Qubays called him: O Ibrahim, this Corner is in me, so take it. He dug it out and placed it. When Ibrahim finished building it, he said: we have done, O Lord, so show us our rites, manifest them to us, teach us them. So Allah sent Jibril, and he performed Hajj with him until he came to Arafah. He said: I have known — and he had come to it once before that — hence it was named Arafah. When it was the day of sacrifice, Satan confronted him, and he said: pelt. So he pelted with seven pebbles, then the second and third days. He blocked what was between the two mountains — meaning cursed Iblis — hence the stoning of the jamarat. He said: climb upon Thabir. He climbed it and called with his loudest voice: O servants of Allah, answer Allah; O servants of Allah, obey Allah. All between the seven seas heard his call, whoever had a mustard-seed of faith in his heart. It is what Allah gave Ibrahim in the rites, His saying: Labbayka Allahumma labbayk, Allahumma labbayka labbayk. There never ceased to be seven Muslims or more upon the earth; were it not so, the earth and those upon it would perish. Ibn Jurayj said: as for Mujahid, he said: Ibrahim mounted his Maqam and said: O servants of Allah, answer Allah; O servants of Allah, obey Allah. Whoever performs Hajj today is of those who answered Ibrahim that day. It is what Allah gave Ibrahim in the rites, His saying: Labbayka Allahumma labbayk. Then Ibrahim built it."
"8889","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ibn Abi Nujayh, from Mujahid, he said: when Ibrahim was commanded to call the people to Hajj, he stood upon the Maqam and said: O servants of Allah, answer Allah. They said: Labbayka, our Lord, labbayk. Whoever performs Hajj is of those who answered the call of Ibrahim."
"8890","From Abu Sa'id, he said: I heard Mujahid say: this Maqam stretched for Ibrahim when Allah said to Ibrahim: proclaim Hajj among the people — until it was the tallest mountain on the earth. He called a call that made heard what is between the seven seas. He said: O servants of Allah, answer Allah; O servants of Allah, obey Allah. They said: Labbayk, O Allah, we answered You; labbayk, O Allah, we obeyed You. He said: whoever performs Hajj until the Hour rises is of those who answered Ibrahim."
"8891","From Ibn Mujahid, from his father, he said: sheep would leap over the back of the House, of stones, because of its lowness, until Ibrahim and Isma'il built it. He said: and they built it fifteen years before pasturage came out to it."
"8892","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Mujahid said: it was a trellis the sheep would leap over, until fifteen years before the mission of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Quraysh built it. A Byzantine trading to Mandal, when at Shu'ayba his ship broke. He sent to Quraysh: come, I will supply you with whatever timber, carpenters and wood you wish, provided you carry it, and you build the house of Ibrahim, provided you carry my trade in your caravans. Quraysh had two journeys every year: in winter to Sham, in summer to Abyssinia. They said: yes. In the House there was a well in which were ornaments and gifts. Quraysh would approve a man for it to be over that well and what was in it. While a man of those approved for it, his soul enticed him to betray. He waited until the shadows ended and the gatherings rose, spread his garment, then descended into it and took, then a second time, then a third. Allah decreed a stone upon him in it, trapping him head-down therein. The people came in the evening and took him out, and he returned what he had taken from it. Then Allah sent a serpent and housed it in it. When it sensed a sound at the door, it raised its head, and no creature of Allah would approach it. When the people needed it, they said: what of the beast in the House? Al-Walid ibn al-Mughira said: gather and supplicate your Lord; if what you intend is pleasing to Allah, He will suffice you against it, otherwise you cannot. He said: they supplicated Allah, and Allah sent a bird that hovered at the door. When the serpent sensed it, it raised its head, and it seized it and went with it like a piece of wood. It says: as if it thought it could hardly carry it, until it ascended a ladder that was in Makkah, and it was never seen after. Quraysh built. When they came to the place of the Corner, the tribes vied. This tribe said: we shall raise it, and this tribe said: we shall raise it. They said: the first man to enter from this upper door shall judge between us. Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered. They said: O Muhammad, judge between us. He said: place a garment, then place it in it, then a man from each tribe shall take it. They did so, and he took the Corner, placing his hand beneath it. He was the one who raised it with them until he placed it with them in its place now."
"8893","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, he said: when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reached maturity, a woman perfumed the Ka'ba with incense, and a spark flew from her censer into the garments of the Ka'ba, and it burned. Quraysh consulted about demolishing it, and they feared demolishing it. Al-Walid ibn al-Mughira said to them: what do you intend by demolishing it — repair or harm? They said: we intend repair. He said: Allah does not destroy the reformer. They said: who shall climb it and demolish it? Al-Walid ibn al-Mughira said: I shall climb it and demolish it. Al-Walid ibn al-Mughira climbed upon the back of the House with the axe, then said: O Allah, we intend only repair. Then he demolished. When Quraysh saw him demolish of it, and nothing they feared befell them, they demolished with him, until when they built and reached the place of the Corner, Quraysh disputed about the Corner, which tribe shall undertake raising it, until they nearly fought. They said: come, we shall arbitrate the first who appears upon us from this alley. They agreed upon that, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appeared to them as a youth, wearing a striped cloak. They arbitrated him. He ordered the Corner placed in a garment, then ordered the chief of each tribe and gave him a side of the garment. Then he climbed, and they raised the Corner to him, and he was the one who placed it."
"8894","Ma'mar reported, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Mujahid, he said: when the House was demolished in Jahiliyya, then they built it, until they reached the place of the Corner, a serpent came out upon them, as if its neck were the neck of a camel. The people feared any man approaching it. He said: a bird came shading half of Makkah, seized it by its leg, then soared with it until it cast it into the sea. Mujahid said: they went out one day on a feast of theirs, and a man pulled a stone from the House, then stole of its ornaments and persisted, then returned to steal, and the two stones stuck upon his head. The people came to him with his head fixed in them."
"8895","From Ma'mar, from Abdullah, from Abu al-Tufayl, he said: the Ka'ba in Jahiliyya was built of loose stones with no clay, low enough for a young goat to leap over, and it was roofless. Its garments were only placed upon it, then draped over it. The Black Corner was placed upon its wall, exposed. It had two corners, like this ring. A ship came from the land of Byzantium, until near Juddah the ship broke. Quraysh went out to take its timber, and they found a Byzantine with it. They took the timber — he gave it to them. The ship was bound for Abyssinia, and the Byzantine in the ship was a carpenter. They brought the timber and brought the Byzantine. Quraysh said: we shall build with this timber the house of our Lord. When they wanted to demolish it, they found a serpent upon the wall of the House like a piece of a beam, black-backed and white-bellied. Whenever one approached the House to demolish it or take of its stones, it rushed to him open-mouthed. Quraysh gathered at the Haram, cried out to Allah, and said: our Lord, we meant no harm; we meant to honor Thy house and arrange it. If Thou art pleased with that, otherwise do as seems to Thee. They heard a rumble in the sky, and there was a bird greater than an eagle, black-backed, white-bellied and legged. It drove its talons into the neck of the serpent, then went with it dragging it, its tail greater than so-and-so, fallen, until it went with it toward Ajyad. Quraysh demolished it and built it with the stones of the valley, Quraysh carrying them upon their necks. They raised it in the sky twenty cubits. While the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) carried stones from Ajyad wearing a striped woolen wrap, the wrap tightened upon him, and he went to place the wrap upon his shoulder, and his nakedness showed because of the smallness of the wrap. He was called: O Muhammad, cover your nakedness. He was never seen naked after that. Between the Ka'ba and what Allah revealed to him were five years, and between his departure and its building fifteen years."
"8896","When the army of al-Husayn ibn Numayr came, he mentioned its burning in the time of Ibn al-Zubayr. Ibn al-Zubayr said: A'isha told me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: were it not for the recentness of your people in disbelief, I would demolish the Ka'ba, for they left seven cubits of it in the Hijr, as means and timber ran short for them."
"8897","Ibn Khuthaym said: Ibn Abi Mulayka told me, from A'isha, that she heard that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I would make for it two doors, eastern and western; they enter from this and exit from this. Ibn al-Zubayr did that. Quraysh had made for it a stair by which whoever came to it climbed; Ibn al-Zubayr made it flush with the ground."
"8898","Ibn Khuthaym said: and Ibn Sabit told me, that Zayd told him, that when Ibn al-Zubayr built it they uncovered the foundations, and there was a stone of them like the she-camel's udder, interlocked with one another; when moved with the crowbar, the one at the other side moved. Ibn Sabit said: I saw Zayd at night after Isha on a moonlit night, and I saw them like udders, the ends of some interlocked with others."
"8899","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, and Kathir ibn Kathir ibn al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wada'a, one adding to the other, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: ask me, O company of youth, for I am about to depart from among you. The people asked him much. A man said to him: may Allah set you right, what of the Maqam — is it as we used to say? He said: what did you say? He said: we used to say that when Ibrahim, peace be upon him, came, the mother of Isma'il offered him lodging, and he refused, and she brought this stone. He said: it is not so. Sa'id said: Ibn Abbas said: the first thing women took, the waist-wrap, was from the mother of Isma'il; she took a waist-wrap to efface her trace from Sara. Then Ibrahim brought her and her son Isma'il, while she nursed him, until he placed them at the House, at a lote-tree above Zamzam in the upper part of the mosque. There was no one in Makkah that day, nor water in it. He placed them there, and placed with them a bag with dates and a waterskin with water. Then Ibrahim turned to depart. The mother of Isma'il followed him and said: O Ibrahim, where are you going, leaving us in this place with no human nor anything? She said that to him repeatedly, and he did not turn to her. She said to him: did Allah command you with this? He said: yes. She said: then He will not let us perish. Then she returned. Ibrahim, peace be upon him, went on until at the pass, where they could not see him, he faced the House with his face, then supplicated with these supplications: Our Lord, I have settled of my offspring in a valley without crops, at Thy Sacred House, that they may give thanks. The mother of Isma'il nursed Isma'il and drank of that water, until when what was in the waterskin ran out, she thirsted and her son thirsted. She looked at him writhing — or he said: struggling — and she went, disliking to look at him. She found al-Safa the nearest mountain to her, stood upon it, then faced the valley looking if she saw anyone, and she saw no one. She descended from al-Safa until when she reached the valley, she raised the edge of her shift and hastened as an exhausted man hastens until she crossed the valley. Then she came to al-Marwa, stood upon it, and looked if she saw anyone, and she saw no one. She did that seven times. Ibn Abbas said: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: hence people run between them. When she looked down upon al-Marwa, she heard a sound. She said: hush — meaning to herself. Then she listened, and heard again. Then she said: Thou hast made me hear; if Thou hast aid... And there was the angel at the site of Zamzam, digging with his heel — or he said: with his wing — until water appeared. She began to dam it thus, saying with her hand, and scooped water into her waterskin, and it gushed as much as she scooped. Ibn Abbas said: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: may Allah have mercy on the mother of Isma'il; had she left Zamzam — or he said: had she not scooped of the water — Zamzam would have been a flowing spring. He said: she drank and nursed her child. The angel said to her: fear no perishing, for here is the House of Allah; this boy and his father shall build it, and Allah does not let His people perish. The House was raised above the ground like a hill; torrents came, taking to its right and left. They remained thus until a party of Jurhum — or a household of Jurhum — passed by them, coming from the way of Kida. They alighted at lower Makkah and saw a circling bird. They said: this bird circles over water; our covenant with this valley is with no water in it. They sent one or two scouts, and they found the water. They returned and told them of the water, while the mother of Isma'il, peace be upon her, was at the water. They said: wilt thou permit us to alight by thee? She said: yes, but you have no right to the water. They said: yes. Ibn Abbas said: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: the mother of Isma'il found that, loving company. They alighted and sent to their families, and they alighted with them, until there were households of them there. The boy grew and learned Arabic from them, and pleased them when he grew. When he came of age they married him a woman of them. The mother of Isma'il died. Ibrahim came after Isma'il had married, to look after his legacy, but did not find Isma'il. He asked his wife about him. She said: he went out seeking for us. Then he asked about their condition, their livelihood. She said: we are in evil, in straitness and hardship, and she complained to him. He said: when your husband comes, convey to him peace, and say to him: change the threshold of thy door. When Isma'il came, it was as if he sensed something. He said: did anyone come to you? She said: yes, an old man so-and-so came to us, asked us about you, and I told him; he asked us about our livelihood, and I told him we are in hardship and strain. He said: did he enjoin anything upon thee? She said: yes, he ordered me to convey peace to thee, and says: change the threshold of thy door. He said: that was my father, and he ordered me to part from thee; join thy family. So he divorced her. Then he married another. Ibrahim stayed from them as Allah willed, then came to them after that, but did not find him. He entered upon his wife and asked about him. She said: he went out seeking for us. He said: how are you? He asked her about their livelihood and condition. She said: well, we are in plenty, and she praised Allah. He said: what is your food? She said: meat. He said: what is your drink? She said: water. He said: O Allah, bless them in meat and water. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that day: there was no grain; had they grain he would have supplicated for them in it. He said: none is alone with them outside Makkah but they did not suit him. He said: when your husband comes, convey to him peace from me, and order him to keep firm the threshold of his door. When Isma'il came, he said: did anyone come to thee? She said: yes, a comely old man came to us, and she praised him; he asked me about thee and I told him; he asked me about our livelihood, and I said: we are well. He said: did he enjoin anything upon thee? She said: he conveys peace to thee, and says to thee: keep firm the threshold of thy house. He said: that was my father, and thou art the threshold; he ordered me to keep thee. Then he stayed from them as Allah willed, then came after that, while Isma'il sharpened arrows of his beneath a lote-tree near Zamzam. When he saw him he rose, and they did as father does with son. Then he said: O Isma'il, Allah commands me to build a house here. He pointed to a mound raised above what is around it, which the torrent comes from its sides but does not rise over it. They stood digging out the foundations; thereupon he raised the foundations of the House. Ibrahim brought stones and Isma'il built, until when the building rose, he brought this stone and placed it for him, and he stood upon it while building, with Isma'il handing to him, both saying: Our Lord, accept from us; Thou art the Hearing, the Knowing. They built until they went round the House, both saying: Our Lord, accept from us; Thou art the Hearing, the Knowing. Ma'mar said: I heard a man say: Ibrahim came to them upon the Buraq. He said: I heard another man say: they wept when they met until the birds answered them. Ma'mar said: Umar ibn al-Khattab said to Quraysh: the guardians of this House before you were Tasm; they slighted it and did not magnify its sanctity, so Allah destroyed them. Then after them Jurhum held it; they slighted it and did not magnify its sanctity, so Allah destroyed them. So do not slight it, and magnify its sanctity."
"8900","From Ibn Uyayna, from al-Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi, he said: when Ibrahim and Isma'il finished the foundations of the House, Ibrahim said to Isma'il: bring me a stone I shall make a marker from which people find guidance. He brought him a stone, but he did not approve it. He said: go, bring me another stone. Ibrahim was brought the Black Stone, and Isma'il came with the stone. Ibrahim said to him: one who did not leave me to thy stone has brought it to me."
"8901","From Ma'mar, he said: it reached me that the Stone remained upon Abu Qubays forty years, like a white thughama plant."
"8902","From Zam'a ibn Salih, he said: Amr ibn Dinar told me, that Ibn Abbas said: drinking Zamzam is by taking the bucket, then facing the qibla, and drinking of it until satiated at the ribs, for no hypocrite is satiated of it at the ribs."
"8903","From Abdullah ibn Umar — and I know Thawri narrated it to us — from Uthman ibn al-Aswad, from Ibn Abi Mulayka, he said: I was with Ibn Abbas when a man came and sat beside him. Ibn Abbas said to him: whence hast thou come? He said: I drank of Zamzam. He said: didst thou drink it as should be? He said: how should be, O Ibn Abbas? He said: thou facest the qibla, namest Allah, then drinkest, breathing three times; when finished thou praisest Allah the Exalted, and drinkest thy fill of it. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: the sign between us and the hypocrites is that they do not drink their fill of Zamzam."
"8904","From al-Thawri, he said: I heard one mention that Ibn Abbas drank of Zamzam, then said: I ask Thee beneficial knowledge, ample provision, and healing from every disease."
"8905","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, that Abd al-Muttalib, when he opened Zamzam, built a basin upon it. He and his son al-Harith drew and filled that basin, giving pilgrims to drink of it. Some envious men of Quraysh broke it by night, and Abd al-Muttalib repaired it in the morning. When they spoiled it much, Abd al-Muttalib supplicated his Lord, and he was shown in a dream. He said: say: O Allah, I do not permit it to one washing, but it is lawful and wholesome for a drinker. Then he was spared them. Abd al-Muttalib said, when Quraysh hastened in the mosque, he called with what he was shown, then departed. None spoiled that basin of his after but was struck with a disease in his body, until they left him his basin and his draught."
"8906","From Ma'mar, he said: Ibn Tawus told me, from his father, he said: one who heard Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib told me, saying while standing at Zamzam, raising his garments with his hand: O Allah, I do not permit it to one washing, but it is — I think he said — and to one abluting, lawful and wholesome. From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he heard Ibn Abbas also say, standing at Zamzam, the like of that."
"8907","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Yazid told me, from Ibrahim ibn Abd Allah ibn Qariz, that Zuyayd ibn al-Salt told him, that Ka'b said of Zamzam: righteous, treasured, treasured for you; the first for whom it gushed was Isma'il. Ka'b said in this hadith: and we find it the food of one fed, and healing of sickness."
"8908","From Zam'a ibn Salih, he said: Salama ibn Wahram narrated to me, he said: one who heard Tubay' told me, saying, from Ka'b, he said: when he entered Zamzam, he entered it with his camel, then drank of it and poured upon his garments. He was said to: why wettest thou thy garments, O Bedouin? He said: you do not know this; this is in the Book of Allah, righteous, the drink of the righteous, Zamzam; it neither runs dry nor is blamed; its name is Rawa; the food of one fed, and healing of sickness."
"8909","From Ibn Uyayna, from Furat al-Qazzaz, from Abu al-Tufayl, from Ali, he said: the best two valleys among men are Dhi Makkah, and a valley in India to which Adam, peace be upon him, descended, wherein is this scent you perfume with; the worst two valleys among men are the valley of al-Ahqaf, and a valley in Hadramawt called Barahut; the best well among men is Zamzam, and the worst well among men is Balahut, a well in Barahut wherein gather the spirits of the disbelievers."
"8910","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard it said: the best water on earth is the water of Zamzam, and the worst water on earth is the water of Barahut, a ravine of the ravines of Hadramawt; the best lands of the earth are the mosques, and the worst lands of the earth are the markets."
"8911","From al-Thawri, from Ibn Khuthaym, or from al-A'la — Abu Bakr doubted — from Abu al-Tufayl, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I heard him say: we used to call it Shuba'a, meaning Zamzam, and we found it an excellent aid for dependents."
"8912","From Ma'mar and al-Thawri, from Ibn Khuthaym, from Wahb ibn Munabbih, he said: we find it in the Book of Allah, meaning Zamzam, the drink of the righteous, meaning Zamzam, treasured, the food of one fed and healing of sickness; it is not drawn dry, nor blamed. He said: Wahb said: whoever drinks of it till satiated at the ribs, it brings him healing and expels disease from him."
"8913","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: Zamzam is the food of one fed and healing of sickness."
"8914","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Khuthaym, that Mujahid used to say: it is for what it is drunk for — meaning: it benefits for what it is drunk for."
"8915","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: Zamzam is for what it is drunk for: if thou drinkest it seeking healing, Allah heals thee; if thou drinkest it seeking to cut thy thirst, it cuts it; if thou drinkest it seeking to sate thee, it sates thee. It is the stroke of Jibril and the draught of Allah for Isma'il."
"8916","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, that he named Zamzam, calling it Zamzam, Barra and Madnuna."
"8917","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: when Ibn al-Zubayr wanted to take the siqaya out of the mosque, Ibn Abbas said to him: thou hast followed neither the piety of one more pious than thee, nor the impiety of one more impious than thee."
"8918","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Abi Husayn narrated to me, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to Suhayl ibn Amr: if my letter comes to thee by night, do not morning, or by day, do not evening, until thou send me water of Zamzam. The wife of Suhayl sought the help of Uthayla al-Khuza'iyya, grandmother of Ayyub ibn Abd Allah ibn Zuhayr. They set out at night with slave-girls with them, and before morning they had filled two waterskins, loaded them, placing them in two Ghawti baskets, then filled them with water, and sent them to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"8919","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: it reached me, from Ka'b, that he said: Isma'il was buried between Zamzam and the Corner and the Maqam."
"8920","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abdullah ibn Uthman told me, from Ibn Sabit, from Abdullah ibn Damra al-Saluli, he said: I circumambulated with him until when we were between the Corner and the Maqam, he mentioned so-and-so, until he mentioned the grave of Isma'il there — I think he mentioned about ninety prophets, or seventy."
"8921","From Ibn Uyayna, from Zuhayr, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr say: this humped place is the grave of the virgin daughters of Isma'il; it is the raised place opposite the door of Banu Sahm toward the Corner."
"8922","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata narrated to me, that Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman told him, from Abu Hurayra, or from A'isha, that she said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: a prayer in my mosque is better than a thousand prayers in other mosques, except the Sacred Mosque. From Ma'mar and Ibn Uyayna, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayra, he said, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said the like of it."
"8923","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata told me, that he heard Ibn al-Zubayr say upon the minbar: a prayer in the Sacred Mosque is better than a hundred prayers in other mosques. He said: he did not name the mosque of Madinah, so it occurs to me he means the mosque of Madinah. From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Sulayman ibn Atiq told me the like of this report of Ata, with Ibn al-Zubayr pointing with his hand toward Madinah."
"8924","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Nafi, freedman of Ibn Umar, say: Ibrahim ibn Abd Allah ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, that Ibn Abbas narrated: that Maymuna, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: a prayer in this mosque of mine is better than a thousand prayers in others, except the mosque of the Ka'ba."
"8925","From Abdullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: a prayer in this mosque of mine is better than a thousand prayers in others, except the Sacred Mosque."
"8926","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: a prayer in the mosque of Madinah is better than a thousand prayers in others, except the Sacred Mosque."
"8927","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: a prayer in this mosque of mine is better than a thousand prayers in others, except the Sacred Mosque."
"8928","From Ma'mar, from Qatada, he said: a prayer in the Sacred Mosque is better than a hundred prayers in Madinah. Ma'mar said: I heard Ayyub narrate, from Abu al-Aliya, from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, the like of the saying of Qatada."
"8929","He said: I heard Ibrahim al-Makki narrate, from Ata, he said: al-Sharid came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of the conquest and said: I vowed, if Allah conquers Makkah for thee, to pray in Bayt al-Maqdis. He said: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: here is better — three times. Then he said: by Him in Whose Hand is my soul, hadst thou prayed here it would suffice thee. Then he said: a prayer in this mosque is better than a thousand prayers in other mosques."
"8930","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told from Ya'qub ibn Mujammi', he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab entered the mosque of Quba and said: by Allah, that I pray in this mosque one prayer is dearer to me than to pray four in Bayt al-Maqdis — after praying in Bayt al-Maqdis one prayer. Had this mosque been in a horizon of horizons, we would strike to it the flanks of camels."
"8931","From al-Aslami, from Salih, freedman of al-Taw'ama, that he heard Abu Hurayra say: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: a prayer in this mosque of mine is better than a thousand prayers in others, except the Sacred Mosque."
"8932","Ibn Jurayj reported, he said: Ata said: if a man spits in the Hijr, no harm if he conceals it."
"8933","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told that Abu Ubayda ibn al-Jarrah spat in the mosque, then went out and did not conceal it. They came with him with a lamp, and he kept picking it up with his cloak, tracing it with it."
"8934","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad, freedman of Aslam, and others: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: whoever spits in the mosque while pure, a sin is written upon him; let one of you conceal his spit."
"8935","From al-Hasan ibn Umara, from al-Hakam, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, he said: he was asked about a man in the Ka'ba wanting to spit. He said: let him spit in his garment."
"8936","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: when the people of Sham were in the first army, the army of al-Husayn ibn Numayr, a man burned from near the door of Banu Jumah, while the mosque that day was full of tents and buildings. The fire spread till it burned the House, burning all upon it, till a bird alighting upon it scattered its stones. Ibn Jurayj said: a Qurashi man called Muhammad ibn al-Murtafi' said to me: by Allah, as we prayed one night Isha behind Ibn al-Zubayr, I saw in the belly of the House — and we saw through the gap of the door. When Ibn al-Zubayr finished, he said: did you see? We said: yes. He said: Ibn al-Zubayr resolved to demolish and build it. He sent to me so-and-so camels carrying wars dye from Yemen — he mentioned four thousand camels — and something he named, wanting to make it mortar for the House. Then he was told wars rots and crumbles, so he distributed the wars among the women and seated women of Quraysh, and built with gypsum. When he prepared his need, Ibn Abbas sent to him: if thou doest, do not leave people with no qibla; place poles on its corners, and place curtains upon them, that people pray to them. He did so, until on Sunday he ascended the minbar, then said: O people, what deem ye of demolishing the House? None differed with him. They said: we deem thou not demolish it. He was silent with them, until when their opinion waned, he said: one of you keeps stopping his fundament upon his head, while you see a bird alight upon it scattering its stones. Lo, I shall demolish tomorrow. That coincided with the funeral of a man of Banu Bakr, which those who would follow it followed and those who would not. A cushion was broken for him at the Maqam. Then men mounted it from behind the curtains. The people finished their funeral; one going to Mina, one going to the well of Maymun, deeming only that a blast from heaven would strike them. When the people came, it was said: enter, for by Allah he demolished. The people entered. He dug till he demolished it down to a foundation in the Hijr — and it held together, inseparable. He called the elders of Quraysh and showed them it. Ibn Muti' took the crowbar from the side of the foundation toward the house of Banu Humayd, and shook it all, entire. Then he built it till he named it, making for it two doors flush with the ground, eastern and western; people enter from this door and exit from this. He built it. When he finished building it, there was in the mosque a great pit, rubble and trespass. The people returned to the valley bed; a man would lay a hundred laden valley camels of it, till a man went out in his suit and shirt to Dhu Tuwa, bringing in the edge of his cloak gravel, seeking good therein, until when the people tired, he took to feeding it, laying gravel till it leveled. He said: O people, I deem ye perform Umra from al-Tan'im walking; whoever is rich, a camel he sacrifices, else a cow, else a sheep. He said: I recalled the Day of Resurrection from the multitude of people; the earth, plain and mountain, crept with people, great and small, virgins, previously married, women, and the shaven. He said: we came to the House, circumambulated with him, ran between al-Safa and al-Marwa, then sacrificed and slaughtered. I never saw heads, trotters and forearms in a place more than that day."
"8937","From Ma'mar, he said: one who saw those foundations moved with the crowbar told me, and the House almost moved. He said: as if they were kneeling camels."
"8938","From Ibn Uyayna, from Hisham ibn Hujayr, from Tawus, or another, from Ibn Abbas, he said: the Hijr is of the House. He said: let them circumambulate the Ancient House. He said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) circumambulated from behind it."
"8939","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr, he said: al-Harith ibn Abd Allah came as delegate to Abd al-Malik in his caliphate. Abd al-Malik said: I do not think Abu Khubayb heard from A'isha what he claimed he heard of her. Al-Harith was truthful, not lying. He said: what didst thou hear her say? He said: I heard her say: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: thy people shortened the building of the House. Were it not for their recent covenant with polytheism, I would restore in it what they left of it. If it occurs to thy people to build it after me, come that I show thee what they left of it. He showed her nearly seven cubits. This is the hadith of Abdullah ibn Ubayd. Al-Walid ibn Ata added upon it, he said: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I would make for it two doors flush with the ground, eastern and western. Dost thou know why thy people raised its door? She said: no. He said: in pride, that they admit none but whom they wish; if they dislike a man entering it, they let him till he climbs, and when he is about to enter, they push him and he falls. Abd al-Malik said to al-Harith: didst thou hear her say this? He said: yes. He tapped with his staff a while, then said: I wish I had left it and what he bore."
"8940","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to A'isha: hast thou not seen thy people shortened from the foundations of Ibrahim? She said: wilt thou not return it, O Messenger of Allah, to the foundations of Ibrahim? He said: thy people are recent in covenant with disbelief — or: they are recent in disbelief."
"8941","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Yazid, he said: my father narrated to me, that Umar came to Makkah and sent to an old man of Banu Zuhra, asking him about a child of Jahili births. The women of Jahiliyya had no waiting period. He said: he told me he went with the old man to Umar, and found him sitting in the Hijr. He asked him. He said: the seed is of so-and-so, but the child is upon the bed of so-and-so. Umar said: thou spoke truth, but the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) judged by the bed. He said: when the old man rose, Umar said: come, tell me of the building of the Ka'ba. He said: Quraysh gathered strength to build the Ka'ba in Jahiliyya, but failed and shortened, leaving part of its building in the Hijr. Umar said: thou spoke truth."
"8942","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr narrate, that between A'isha and her brother Abd al-Rahman was something, and he swore not to speak to her. She wanted him to come to her, but he refused. She was told: he has an hour of the night he circumambulates. She awaited him at the door of the Hijr, until when he passed her, she seized his garment, then dragged him till she entered the Hijr. Then she said: so-and-so is free for thee, and so-and-so is free for thee, and by Him in Whose House I am... She kept swearing to him and apologizing to him."
"8943","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Kathir ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, from Umm Kulthum bint Amr ibn Abi Aqrab, from A'isha, that she asked him to open the Ka'ba for her by night, but he refused her — they claim Shayba ibn Uthman. A'isha said to Umm Kulthum: go, enter the Ka'ba. So she entered the Hijr."
"8944","From Ma'mar, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from A'isha, she said: I care not whether I prayed in the Hijr or in the belly of the House."
"8945","From Ma'mar, from one of his companions, that A'isha prayed in the Hijr and said: I shall surely pray in the House — meaning the Hijr — though the nose of so-and-so be rubbed in dust — for one of the gatekeepers; he had barred her from entering the House by night."
"8946","My father told me, he said: I heard Marthad ibn Shurahbil narrate, that he was present at that. He said: Ibn al-Zubayr brought to A'isha seventy men of the best of Quraysh and their elders, and she told them that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: were it not for the recent covenant of thy people with polytheism, I would build the House upon the foundations of Ibrahim and Isma'il. Dost thou know why they fell short of the foundations of Ibrahim? She said: no. He said: means fell short for them. He said: the Ka'ba had weakened from the fire of the people of Sham. He said: he demolished it, while I that day was in Makkah. He uncovered a foundation in the Hijr, holding together. He left it uncovered eight days to bear witness upon it. He said: I saw that foundation of it like the flanks of camels, five stones: a face a stone, and a face two stones. He said: I saw a man take the crowbar and shake it from the side of the Corner, and the other Corner shook. He said: then he built upon that foundation, making two doors flush with the ground, eastern and western. When Ibn al-Zubayr was killed, al-Hajjaj demolished it from the side of the Hijr, then restored it as it was. Abd al-Malik wrote to him: I wish thou hadst left Ibn al-Zubayr and what he bore. He said: Marthad said: I heard Ibn Abbas say: had I charge of it what Ibn al-Zubayr had of the Hijr, I would enter the whole Hijr into the House; why is it circumambulated if it is not of the House?"
"8947","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayra, he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: saddles are tied to three mosques: the Sacred Mosque, this mosque of mine, and al-Aqsa Mosque."
"8948","From Ma'mar, from a man of Ghifar, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id, he said: a man of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was met. It was said: whence hast thou come? He said: from al-Tur. He said: had I met thee, I would not have let thee go. Then he narrated to him, he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: saddles are tied to three mosques: the Sacred Mosque, al-Aqsa Mosque, and this mosque of mine."
"8949","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar told me, from Talq ibn Habib, that Ibn Umar used to say: saddles are tied to three mosques: the Sacred Mosque, the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and al-Aqsa Mosque. Ibn Jurayj said: and I say: Ibn Ata used to say: saddles are tied to three mosques, mentioning the like. Ata denied al-Aqsa, then returned and counted it with them."
"8950","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Tawus said: saddles are moved to two mosques: the mosque of Makkah and the mosque of Madinah."
"8951","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told from Busra ibn Abi Busra, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: mounts are worked only to three mosques: the Sacred Mosque, then the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the mosque of Bayt al-Maqdis."
"8952","From al-Thawri, from Ya'qub ibn Mujammi' ibn Jariya, from his father, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab came and said: had the mosque of Quba been in a horizon of horizons, we would strike to it the livers of mounts."
"8953","From Ma'mar, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: while Umar was among camels of sadaqa camels, two men passed him. He said: whence have ye come? They said: from Bayt al-Maqdis. He beat them with the stick, saying: a Hajj like the Hajj of the House? They said: O Commander of the Faithful, we came from such-and-such land, passing by it, and prayed in it. He said: so then — and he left them."
"8954","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Uyayna, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: a man came, seeking Umar's leave to Bayt al-Maqdis. Umar said: equip; when finished, inform me. When he finished, he came to him. He said: make it an Umra."
"8955","Al-Thawri reported, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, from Shaqiq, he said: Ibn Mas'ud said: had there been between me and Bayt al-Maqdis two farsakhs, I would not come to it."
"8956","From al-Thawri, he said: Jabir narrated to me, he said: I heard al-Sha'bi swear by Allah: Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned away from Bayt al-Maqdis only in displeasure — meaning toward Bayt al-Maqdis."
"8957","My father told me, he said: I said to al-Muthanna: I want to come to Madinah. He said: do not do. I heard Ata, when a man asked him, say to him: seven circuits of the House are better than thy journey to Madinah."
"8958","From a companion of his, he said: I said to al-Thawri: I want to come to Madinah. He said: do not do."
"8959","One who heard Ata told me, saying: seven circuits are better for thee than thy journey to Madinah. I said: shall I come to Juddah? He said: no; you were only commanded with tawaf. He said: I said: shall I go out to al-Shajara and perform Umra from it? He said: no. He said: one of the scholars said: my feet have not ceased since I came to Makkah. He said: I said: is going back and forth dearer to thee, or residence? He said: rather going back and forth."
"8960","From Ibn Uyayna, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Arfaja, he said: I said to Ibn Umar: I want to come to al-Tur. He said: saddles are tied only to three mosques: the Sacred Mosque, the mosque of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and al-Aqsa Mosque. Leave al-Tur; come not to it."
"8961","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muhammad ibn Ali told me, he said: I was told that whoever looks at the House, magnifying it and knowing its right, a good deed is written for him thereby, and a misdeed effaced from him thereby. Whoever comes to it visiting it, magnifying it and knowing it, his sins fall when he looks at it, as leaves fall from the tree."
"8962","From Ibn Mujahid, from Ata and Mujahid, they said: looking at the House is worship; a good deed is written for him thereby, and the angels pray upon him so long as he looks at it. From Ibn Mubarak, he said: Aban ibn Abd Allah al-Bajali told me, from Ata, the like of it."
"8963","From Ma'mar, from Ibn al-Munkadir, he said: it reached me that for every glance cast at the House is a good deed."
"8964","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayra, he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: at the end of time Dhu al-Suwayqatayn shall appear against the Ka'ba. He said: I think he said: and he demolishes it. Ma'mar said: it reached me from some of them: the Ka'ba is demolished three times; it is raised in the third or fourth. So enjoy of it."
"8965","From Ibn Jurayj, from Salih, freedman of al-Taw'ama, that he heard Abu Hurayra — he raised it, I think — he said: leave the Abyssinians as they leave you, for none shall extract the treasure of the Ka'ba but Dhu al-Suwayqatayn of Abyssinia."
"8966","Hisham ibn Hassan reported, from Hafsa bint Sirin, from Abu al-Aliya, that Ali ibn Abi Talib said: much of this tawaf of the House before ye are barred from it, for I see one deaf, bald, mounting it, demolishing it with his spade."
"8967","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Sulayman al-Ahwal narrate, from Mujahid and others, that Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As said: as if I look at him, small-bald, small-splay-footed, mounting it with his spade. Ibn Jurayj said: I heard others of his shaykhs and townspeople: the Abyssinians shall ruin it."
"8968","From Ibn Uyayna, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, he said: as if I look at him, small-bald, small-splay-footed, standing upon it with his spade. Mujahid said: I looked when Ibn al-Zubayr demolished it, while it was demolished, if I saw his description — from Abd al-Razzaq, from Amr — and I did not see it."
"8969","From Ibn Uyayna, from Dawud ibn Shabur, from Mujahid, he said: when Ibn al-Zubayr wanted to demolish it, we fled Makkah, remaining three days, fearing torment would descend upon us."
"8970","From Ma'mar, he said: it reached me that when al-Husayn ibn Numayr set the catapult upon the Ka'ba, a white cloud rose toward Abu Qubays, thundered, then thunderbolted, burning the catapult, and seventy men burned beneath it."
"8971","From al-Thawri, from Salama ibn Kuhayl, from Abu Sadiq, from Ulaym al-Kindi, he said: I heard Salman say: this House shall surely be ruined at the hand of a man of the progeny of Ibn al-Zubayr."
"8972","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba, from Ka'b, that he said: of the Ka'ba — ye demolish it, O Umma, three times, then it is raised in the fourth. So enjoy of it."
"8973","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abdullah ibn Uthman told me, that Sa'id ibn Mina told him, he said: I circumambulated the House with Abdullah ibn Amr after the burning of the House, when he said: O Sa'id, didst thou magnify what befell the House? He said: I said: what is greater than it? He said: the blood of the Muslim shed without right."
"8974","From Hushaym, from Ya'la ibn Ata, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad, he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: whoever looks at his Muslim brother a look frightening him thereby, Allah shall frighten him on the Day of Resurrection."
"8975","I said to Ma'mar, he said: I said to al-Zuhri: has it reached thee that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ibrahim made Makkah sacred, and I make Madinah sacred? He said: I heard of that, but it reached me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: men did not make Makkah sacred, but Allah made it sacred; it is sacred till the Day of Resurrection. Of the most insolent of men against Allah on the Day of Resurrection is a man who kills in the Haram, a man who kills other than his killer, and a man who takes by the blood-claims of Jahiliyya."
"8976","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hasan ibn Muslim told me, from Mujahid, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood on the day of the conquest and said: Allah made Makkah sacred the day He created the heavens and the earth; it is sacred by the sacredness of Allah till the Day of Resurrection. It was not lawful for anyone before me, nor for anyone after me; it was never lawful for anyone ever, but an hour of time. It is sacred by the sacredness of Allah till the Day of Resurrection. Its game is not startled, its thorn not cut, its herbage not uprooted, and its foundling not lawful but for an announcer. Al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib said: except idhkhir, O Messenger of Allah, for it is needful, for the smith and houses. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was silent, then said: except idhkhir, it is lawful. From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr said: I heard Ubayd ibn Umayr mention this all, adding in it: its secure one is not frightened. From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abd al-Karim told me of this sermon of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from Mujahid — or he said: I heard Ikrima mention, from Ibn Abbas."
"8977","From Ma'mar, from Ayyub, from Mujahid: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he entered the mosque on the day of the conquest, ordered those idols — I think he said: around the Ka'ba — and they were knocked upon their faces. Then he ordered them dragged till taken out of the Sacred Mosque, saying: truth has come and falsehood vanished; falsehood is ever vanishing. He said: then he preached, then said: Allah made Makkah sacred the day He created the heavens and the earth; it is sacred by the sacredness of Allah till the Day of Resurrection. It was not lawful for anyone before me nor anyone after me; Allah made it lawful for me but an hour of the day. Its game is not startled, its thorn not cut, its herbage not uprooted, and its foundling not lawful but for an announcer. He said: al-Abbas said: except idhkhir, O Messenger of Allah, for it is for our houses, our goldsmiths and our smiths? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: except idhkhir, it is lawful."
"8978","Ma'mar reported, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Abbas, he said: the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said — I think on the day of the conquest: its herbage is not uprooted, its game not startled, its thorny trees not cut, and its foundling not lawful but for an announcer. Al-Abbas said: except idhkhir, O Messenger of Allah? He said: except idhkhir."
"8979","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata said to me of the lote-tree: it is killed in the Haram — a cow — meaning: it is cut."
"8980","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muzahim ibn Siba' told me, that Abdullah ibn Amir cut the lote-trees of a wall in a ravine of Mina, and trees, and salam, paying for every lote-tree a cow."
"8981","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Abi Najih, he said: for the lote-tree, five or six dinars, given in charity in Makkah."
"8982","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Isma'il ibn Umayya say: Khalid ibn Mudarris told me, that a man of the pilgrims cut trees in his dwelling in Mina — or he said: a tree. He said: I went with him to Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz and told him his news. He said: he spoke truth; they had straitened our dwellings and houses. He said: Umar raged at him, then naught but his religion..."
"8983","From Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr, that Umar ibn al-Khattab saw a man uprooting a samura, and said: its thorny trees are not cut."
"8984","Ma'mar reported, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, of the siwak taken from the Haram: he saw no harm in it."
"8985","From Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: no harm in the siwak and sticks thou takest from the Haram. He said: Ata disliked it."
"8986","From Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: no harm in taking mays, daghabis and siwak of bashama in the Haram. He said: we do not see by his saying: its herbage is not uprooted, but for livestock. Amr said: and by senna leaves for a purge — by my life, if it be of its root most reaching, it shall surely be uprooted as daghabis and nuhays are uprooted of it. As for trade, no."
"8987","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata disliked for me what grows upon thy water in the Haram, of trees of the Haram. Ikrima reviewed Ata and said: if what grows upon my water in the Haram be unlawful for me, my cotton shall surely be unlawful for me, for the ghariba grows in it, and green herbs and herbage grow in it; then people cannot have their greens. He said: what grows upon thy water is lawful for thee, though thou didst not grow it."
"8988","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata disliked for me that I bring near to my camel a branch, or to my sheep. He said: and I say: thou standest surety if thou breakest it, and that is uprooting. Ibn Jurayj said: Ibn Abi Husayn asked him — meaning Ata — he said: my mat is spread upon a house in the Haram, and they alight upon it? He said: yes."
"8989","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata told me, that Umar, while preaching at Mina, saw a Yemeni man cutting of trees. He sent to him and said: what doest thou? He said: I cut fodder for my camel; I have no fodder. He said: knowest thou where thou art? He said: no. He said: Umar ordered for him maintenance."
"8990","From Ma'mar, from Ibn Abi Najih: he disliked that stones of the Haram be taken and made loops for sacks, tied upon them."
"8991","From Umar ibn Habib, he said: Mujahid disliked that anything of the stones of the Haram be taken out."
"8992","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Mansur ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me, from Muhammad ibn Abbad ibn Jafar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not cut the green growth of Uranah and Namirah."
"8993","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade cutting down trees; he said: It is a vital reserve for beasts in time of drought."
"8994","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was informed, from al-Hasan, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not cut down trees, for it is a protection for livestock in time of drought."
"8995","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ata used to forbid charging rent in the Haram, and he informed me that Umar ibn al-Khattab used to forbid that the houses of Makkah be fitted with doors, so that the pilgrims might alight in their courtyards. The first one to fit his house with a door was Suhayl ibn Amr, so Umar ibn al-Khattab sent to him about that, and he said: Grant me time, O Commander of the Believers, I was a merchant and wanted to make two doors to guard my goods. He said: Then that is allowed."
"8996","From Mamar, from Mansur, from Mujahid, that Umar ibn al-Khattab said: O people of Makkah, do not make doors for your houses, so that the desert-dweller may alight wherever he wishes. He said: Mansur informed me, from Mujahid, he said: He forbade letting the houses of Makkah for hire and selling its quarters. He said: And Mamar informed me, and some people of Makkah informed me, he said: Muawiyah came to power while no house in Makkah had a door. Mamar said: And one who heard Ata informed me, he says: The resident therein and the visitor are equal; he said: They alight wherever they wish."
"8997","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I read a letter from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz to Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd Allah, ordering him that nothing in Makkah be let for hire."
"8998","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hujayr informed me, from Tawus, he said: Allah knows that I asked him about a dwelling of mine, and he said: Eat up its rent. Ibn Jurayj said: And Amr ibn Dinar saw no harm in it. He said: And how can there be harm in it when a quarter is sold and its price is consumed, and Umar ibn al-Khattab bought the prison house for four thousand dinars. From Abd al-Rahman ibn Farrukh; and al-Thawri said, from his father, from Nafi ibn Abd al-Harith: He bought the prison house from Safwan ibn Umayyah for three thousand; if Umar approves, the sale is his sale, and if Umar does not approve the sale, Safwan gets four hundred dirhams. Then Umar took it."
"8999","From Ibn Mujahid, from his father, from Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn al-As, he said: The sale of the houses of Makkah and letting them for hire is not lawful."
"9000","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ismail ibn Umayyah, he said: It reached me that Aishah asked permission of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to make a privy at Mina, and he did not permit her."
"9001","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, he said: It is written on the Maqam: Allah's Sacred House at Makkah; its people's dwellings are in water and meat; Allah has guaranteed the provision of its people; it comes to it by three routes: the upper valley, its lower part, and the pass. The first one to settle it does not render it lawful to violate."
"9002","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibrahim ibn Maysarah informed me that he heard Tawus reporting, from Ibn Abbas, he said: It is written on the Maqam: Allah's Sacred House is blessed for its people in meat and water; upon Allah is the provision of its people by three routes. The first one to settle it does not render it lawful to violate."
"9003","From Mamar and Ibn Jurayj, from Kathir ibn Kathir, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas: That the wife of Ismail said to Ibrahim - Ibn Jurayj said in his hadith: She said to Ibrahim: Alight and we will feed you. Ibrahim said: And what is your food? She said: Meat. He said: And what is your drink? She said: Water. He said: O Allah, bless them in meat and water. He said: These two are such that no one stays with them away from Makkah but they do not suit him."
"9004","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, he said: It reached me that they found in the Maqam of Ibrahim three slabs, in each slab a writing. In the first slab: I am Allah, the Lord of Bakka; I made it the day I made the sun and the moon, and I surrounded it with seven upright angels, and I blessed its people in meat and milk. And written in the second slab: I am Allah, the Lord of Bakka; I created the womb-tie, and derived for it a name from My name; whoever joins it I join him, and whoever cuts it I cut him off. And in the third slab: I am Allah; I created good and evil, so blessed is he at whose hands good comes, and woe to him at whose hands evil comes."
"9005","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Mujahid said: It is written on the stone: I am Allah, the Lord of Bakka; I made it the day I made the sun and the moon; I surrounded it with seven upright angels; blessed for its people in meat and milk; and the first to settle it does not render it lawful to violate. And he said: It shall not pass away until the two Akhshabs pass away; and the two Akhshabs are the two great mountains."
"9006","He said: Mamar narrated to us, from a man, from Mujahid, he said: There was found in a stone at Makkah: I am Allah, the Lord of Bakka; I made it the day I made the sun and the moon; and it shall not pass away until the two Akhshabs pass away; I blessed its people in clarified butter and fat; its provision comes to it by three routes; and I surrounded it with seven upright angels. The first to render it lawful to violate for its people."
"9007","From al-Thawri, from Uthman ibn al-Aswad, he said: I heard Mujahid saying: Selling food in Makkah is deviation from the truth."
"9008","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibrahim informed me, raising it to Fatimah al-Sahmiyyah, from Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn al-As, he said: Deviation in the Haram is wronging the servant and anything above that."
"9009","From al-Thawri, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Ibn Sabit, he said: There shall not dwell in it a shedder of blood, nor a dealer in usury, nor a goer about with tale-bearing."
"9010","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: And whoever enters it shall be safe. He said: Everything that enters it is safe. He said: And if he shed blood therein? He said: Except that he killed in the Haram, then he is killed therein. He said: And he recited: At the Sacred Mosque, until they fight you therein. Then if he killed elsewhere and entered it, he is safe until he leaves it. Then he said to me: Ibn Abbas disapproved of Ibn al-Zubayr's killing of Sad, the freedman of Utbah, and his companions. He said: He left him in the open ground, then when he entered the Haram he brought him out of it and killed him. Sulayman ibn Musa said to him: Then a runaway slave enters it? He said: Seize him, for you do not seize him in order to kill him."
"9011","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, about His saying: He shall be safe. He said: Whoever kills or steals in the open ground and then enters the Haram, he is not sat with, nor spoken to, nor sheltered, but he is urged to leave, then what he incurred is carried out against him. And if he killed or stole in the open ground and was brought into the Haram and they wanted to carry out against him what he incurred, they bring him out of the Haram to the open ground, and it is carried out against him. And if he killed in the Haram or stole, it is carried out against him in the Haram."
"9012","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: Ibn Abbas blamed Ibn al-Zubayr concerning a man seized in the open ground, then brought into the Haram, then brought out to the open ground and killed. He said: He brought him into the Haram and then brought him out; meaning: He brought him in under safety. And the man was one whom Ibn al-Zubayr suspected in some affair, and he aided Abd al-Malik against him. And Ibn Abbas did not consider killing due against him. He said: And Ibn al-Zubayr did not remain after him but a little until he perished."
"9013","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I heard Ibn Abi Husayn narrating, from Ikrimah ibn Khalid, he said: Umar said: If I found therein the killer of al-Khattab, I would not touch him until he leaves it."
"9014","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr said: Ibn Umar said: If I found therein the killer of Umar, I would not startle him."
"9015","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: It reached us that Tubba marched to the Kabah intending to demolish it, and the rabbis of the Jews marched with him, until when they were at Marr or Sarif - and some of the learned men say: He reached al-Tanim - the land darkened over them. So he called the rabbis and asked them, and they said: Did you harbor anything in yourself against this House? He said: Yes, I harbored demolishing it. They said: That is why this darkness came. So Tubba covenanted with Allah that if that darkness were lifted from him, he would honor the Kabah and clothe it. So Allah lifted that darkness. Then Tubba marched until, when he reached the marks of the Haram, he alighted from his mount, then took off his sandals in honor of the Haram and in repentance for what he intended. He said: Until he entered Makkah walking barefoot. Then he circumambulated the House and clothed the Kabah with al-Wasail, and it was covered with them. Then he lodged his baggage and kitchen in the ravine of Abd Allah ibn Amir ibn Kariz, so it was called al-Matabikh from that day to this day of the people. And he lodged his weapons in the ravine of Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, so it was called Buqayqian from that day to this day of the people. And he lodged his horses in the ravine of Banu Makhzum, so that ravine was called Ajyad the smaller and Ajyad the greater to this day of the people. And they mentioned that only two men of Hudhayl pointed him to demolish the Kabah; then when Allah lifted that darkness, Tubba ordered the two, and they were brought out of the Haram and crucified. And some of our learned men claimed that the first to clothe the Kabah was Ismail the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Allah knows best about that. And I heard my father narrating, from some of their elders, the like of it."
"9016","From Mamar, from Asim, from Abd Allah ibn Sarjis, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he went out traveling, used to say: O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the hardship of travel, and the gloom of return, and from decrease after increase, and the evil sight in family and wealth. Then Muhammad ibn Thawr said to Mamar: What is decrease after increase, O Abu Urwah? He said: That he not be a gainer; meaning: He was a righteous man, then he turned back on his heels."
"9017","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me that Ali al-Azdi informed him that Ibn Umar taught him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he sat upright upon his camel going out to travel, said takbir three times, then said: Glory to Him who subjected this to us, up to: And to our Lord we shall return. O Allah, we ask You in this journey of ours piety and righteousness, and of deeds what You approve. O Allah, make easy for us this journey of ours, and fold for us its distance. O Allah, You are the Companion in travel and the Successor over the family. O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the hardship of travel, and the distress of return, and the evil sight in the family. And when he returned, he said them, and added therein: Returning, repentant, worshipping, praising our Lord."
"9018","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, he said: They used to say when they went out traveling, they would say: Our Lord, convey to us Your forgiveness and pleasure; in Your hand is good; indeed You are over all things capable. O Allah, You are the Companion in travel and the Successor over the aged and the family. O Allah, make travel easy for us and fold the land for us. O Allah, we seek refuge in You from the hardship of travel and the gloom of return."
"9019","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Abu Ayyub al-Thaqafi, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Tawus, he said: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: Praise be to Allah who created me when I was nothing remembered. O Allah, aid me against the terrors of the world, and the calamities of time, and the misfortunes of nights and days. O Allah, accompany me in my travel, and remain behind me over my family. And to You, so guide me gently; and that upon good character, so make me upright; and to You, O Lord, so make me beloved to You; and to people, so do not entrust me to them. Lord of the oppressed, You are the Lord. I seek refuge in Your noble Face, by which the light of the heavens and the earth shone, and by which You dispelled darknesses, and by which You set right the affair of the first and the last, lest You visit upon me Your anger, or send down upon me Your wrath. To You is amends from me as much as I can; there is no might nor power except in Allah."
"9020","From Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he returned from a journey and passed by a rugged land or a rise of earth, said takbir three times, then said: There is no god but Allah alone, with no partner; to Him belongs dominion and to Him belongs praise, and He is over all things capable. Then he said: Returning, repentant, worshipping, prostrating, praising our Lord. Allah fulfilled His promise, and aided His servant, and defeated the confederates alone."
"9021","From Mamar, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Mujahid, he said: I accompanied Ibn Umar on a journey, and when dawn rose he would raise his voice, saying: A listener has heard Allah's praise, and His mercy, and His good trial upon us. O Allah, accompany us and bestow upon us, seeking refuge in You from the Fire."
"9022","From Umar ibn Dharr, from Yazid al-Faqir, that Ibn Umar, in the morning time while traveling, said - he said several times: A listener has heard Allah's praise and His blessing upon us. O Allah, accompany us and bestow upon us, seeking refuge in Allah from Hell."
"9023","From Mamar, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he went out traveling for Hajj or Umrah and passed by a rugged land or a rise, said takbir three times. Then he mentioned the hadith of Abd Allah ibn Umar."
"9024","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Husayn, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Upon every camel hump is a devil, so remember Allah as you were commanded, then put them to work for yourselves, and Allah carries upon them."
"9025","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara ibn Azib, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he returned from a journey, said: Returning, repentant, worshipping, praising our Lord."
"9026","From Ibrahim ibn Yazid, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he returned from a journey, said: Returning, repentant, God willing, worshipping, God willing, praising our Lord. O Allah, we seek refuge in You from the hardship of travel, and the gloom of return, and the evil sight in family and wealth."
"9027","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Salim, he said: They used to say when they came back from Hajj or Umrah: Returning, God willing, repentant, worshipping, prostrating, praising our Lord. Allah fulfilled His promise, and aided His servant, and defeated the confederates alone. From Ibrahim ibn Yazid, he said: Abu al-Zubayr informed me, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, the like of it."
"9028","From al-Thawri, from Asim ibn Sulayman, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Abu Musa al-Ashari, he said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, and we overlooked a valley, so the people raised their voices; the people began saying takbir and tahlil. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Be gentle with yourselves; you do not call upon one deaf nor absent; He is Hearing, Near; He is with you."
"9029","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his armies, when they ascended the passes, said takbir, and when they descended, said tasbih. The prayer was laid down upon that."
"9030","He said: Mamar informed us, from Ayyub and Asim, or one of them, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, from Abu Musa al-Ashari, he said: The people used to say takbir when they ascended the passes, and when they descended they raised their voices very loudly. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You do not call upon one deaf nor absent, but you call upon One Hearing, Seeing; He is with you. And he ordered them to be calm."
"9031","Hisham ibn Hassan informed us, from al-Hasan: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you are in fertile land, let the beasts have their humps, and do not pass by the halting places; and when you are in barren land, then travel on. And upon you is traveling by night, for the earth is folded at night. And do not alight upon the main roads, for they are the abode of snakes and beasts of prey. And beware of relieving yourselves upon them, for they are among the cursed places. And when the ghouls appear to you, then call the adhan."
"9032","From al-Aslami, from Ibn al-Munkadir, he said: The ghouls were mentioned before Ibn Abbas, and he said: That is a generation that has perished."
"9033","From al-Thawri, from al-Shaybani, from Usayr ibn Amr, he said: The ghouls were mentioned before Umar, and he said: Nothing changes from the form in which it was created, but among them are sorcerers like your sorcerers; so when you see anything of that, then call the adhan."
"9034","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Asim, from Abu al-Udays, from Umar, he said: Disperse away from the place of death, and make one head into two heads; and do not linger in a land of helplessness; and set right your resting places; and frighten the vermin before they frighten you."
"9035","From al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Aban ibn Salih, from Khalid ibn Madan, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah is Gentle, loves gentleness, approves it, and aids upon it what He does not aid upon harshness. So when you ride these dumb beasts, alight with them at their halting places; and if the land is barren, then press on upon them with their marrow. And upon you is traveling by night, for the earth is folded at night as it is not folded by day. And beware of stopping to rest upon the road, for it is the path of beasts and the abode of snakes."
"9036","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I was told, from Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: When the ghouls appear to you, then call the adhan."
"9037","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Muslim al-Batin, he said: Umar said: When one of you buys a camel, let him buy it tall and huge; for if he misses its goodness, he will not miss its price. And do not dress your women in fine linen, for if it does not show through, it describes. And set right your resting places, and frighten the vermin before they frighten you; for no Muslim is exposed from it."
"9038","From Ibn al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Uthman, from Salman, he said: If people knew Allah's bearing for the weak, they would not overprice mounts."
"9039","From al-Aslami, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Travel is but a portion of torment; it deprives one of you of his food and drink. So when one of you finishes his need in his course, let him hasten the return to his family."
"9040","From Thawr ibn Yazid, from Muhajir ibn Habib al-Zubaydi, he said: Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman and Said ibn Jubayr met, and Said said to Abu Salamah: Narrate, for we shall follow you. Abu Salamah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When there are three on a journey, let the most reciting of them lead them, even if he is the youngest of them; then when he leads them, he is their commander. Abu Salamah said: So that is a commander whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) appointed."
"9041","From al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, he said: When you go out traveling, pray two rakahs in your house; and when you come from your journey, pray two rakahs in your house."
"9042","From Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he arrived from a journey, prayed two rakahs in the mosque."
"9043","From al-Thawri, from Malik, from Mighwal, from Yusayr al-Ijli, that Ibn Abbas arrived from a journey and prayed two rakahs upon a mat in his house."
"9044","From Ibn Ajlan, from Yaqub ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Ashajj, from Ibn al-Musayyib, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever alights at a place and says: I seek refuge in the perfect words of Allah, all of them, from the evil of what He created - nothing shall harm him until he departs from it. He said: And as for Malik, he mentioned it from Yaqub ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Ashajj, from Busr ibn Said, from Sad, from Khawlah bint Hakim, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"9045","Jafar ibn Sulayman informed us, from Said al-Jurayri, he said: It reached me that whoever reads this verse: Praise be to Allah who took no son, and has no partner in dominion - to the end of the surah - no theft shall befall him. He said: I heard my father, when he alighted at a place, saying while upon his mount: We have alighted at the best abode, and its best is for the alighter, by praise of the Lord of caravans; He made it righteous and guarded it; He fed it to satiety and gave it drink. And he kept saying it until he finished untying."
"9046","From Abd Allah ibn Kathir, from Shubah, he said: Hamzah, a man of Banu Dabbah, informed me, he said: I heard Anas saying: When we alighted at a place, we kept glorifying Allah until the mounts were untied."
"9047","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Husayn, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Upon every camel hump is a devil; so when you ride, remember Allah as you were commanded, then put them to work for yourselves, and Allah carries upon them. From Mamar, he said: One who heard Tawus narrated to me, he says: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said the like of it."
"9048","From Mamar, he said: It reached me that some people were on a journey, and they did not pray in congregation, nor did they alight properly at the halting place, so their sights were effaced. Then al-Khidr (peace be upon him) appeared to them, and they told him of their affair, so he prayed for them, and Allah restored their sights to them."
"9049","Al-Thawri informed us, from Layth, from one who heard Ibn Umar saying to the pilgrim when he arrived: May Allah magnify your reward - or: May He exalt your reward - and accept your rites, and replace your expenditure for you."
"9050","Mamar informed us, from al-Amash, from Zayd ibn Wahb: When you are three on a journey, appoint one of you; and when you pass by a shepherd, call out three times; then if anyone answers you, else alight and untie, and milk and drink, then tie up the udders."
"9051","From Mamar, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: Umar said: Travel, you will be healthy and provided."
"9052","From Mamar, I think from al-Zuhri - Ibn al-Arabi doubted - from Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) liked to go out on Thursday when he wanted to travel."
"9053","Abu Said Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ziyad ibn Bishr informed us, he said: Abu Yaqub Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Dabari narrated to us, he said: From Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Is raiding obligatory upon all the people? Then he and Amr ibn Dinar said: We do not know."
"9054","From Ibn Jurayj, he said: Dawud ibn Abi Asim informed me: Is raiding obligatory upon all the people? Then he was silent, and he knew that had he denied what I said, he would have made it clear to me. Then I said to Ibn al-Musayyib: I equipped myself, nothing held me back but that, until I guarded the frontier. He said: It has sufficed you."
"9055","From Mamar, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, he said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I am a cowardly man, I cannot bear meeting the enemy. Then he said: Shall I not guide you to a striving in which there is no fighting? He said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Upon you is Hajj and Umrah. From Ibn Jurayj, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, he said: I was informed that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Mamar."
"9056","From Said ibn Abd al-Aziz, he said: I heard Makhul saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no household from which no warrior goes out, nor do they equip a warrior, nor take his place over his family, but Allah strikes them with a calamity before death."
"9057","From Ismail ibn Abd Allah, from Ibn Awn, from Ishaq ibn Suwayd, from Hurayth, he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab saying: Three journeys are incumbent upon you: Hajj and Umrah, and striving in the path of Allah, and that a man seek with the surplus of his wealth. And the spender and the giver of charity. And he says: Upon you is Hajj and Umrah and striving."
"9058","From al-Thawri, from Abu Hayyan and others, he said: Hajj and Umrah are incumbent upon you; meaning: Upon you is Hajj and striving."
"9059","From Ibrahim ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith, from Makhul, from Abu Umamah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Upon you is striving in the path of Allah, for it is a gate among the gates of Paradise; Allah removes thereby deceit and grief."
"9060","From Ibn al-Taymi, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, he said: al-Hawari ibn Ziyad narrated to me, he said: I was sitting with Abd Allah ibn Umar when a young man came to him and said: Will you not strive? Then he was silent and turned away from him. Then Ibn Umar said: Islam is built upon four pillars: establishing prayer, and giving zakat - no separation between the two - and fasting the month of Ramadan, and Hajj to the House for whoever can find a way to it. And striving and charity are among good deeds."
"9061","From Mamar and al-Thawri, from Abu Ishaq, from Silah ibn Zufar, from Hudhayfah, he said: Islam is built upon eight shares: testifying that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, and establishing prayer, and giving zakat, and fasting Ramadan, and Hajj, and enjoining good, and forbidding evil. And he has failed who has no share."
"9062","From a man, from Makhul, that he used to face the qiblah, then swear ten oaths that raiding is obligatory upon you, then say: If you wish, I shall add for you. Abd al-Razzaq said: And I heard al-Awzai - or I was informed of him - that he heard it from Makhul."
"9063","From al-Thawri, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Abis ibn Rabiah, from Umar, he said: When you lay down the saddles, then bind the mounts toward Hajj and Umrah, for it is one of the two strivings."
"9064","From al-Thawri, from Muawiyah ibn Ishaq, from Abayah ibn Rifah, from al-Husayn ibn Ali, he said: A man asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about striving, and he said: Shall I not guide you to a striving in which there is no harm? Hajj."
"9065","From al-Thawri, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Abu al-Abbas, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, he said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I want to strive. Then he said: Are your parents alive? He said: Yes. He said: Then in them is striving."
"9066","From al-Thawri, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from his father, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, he said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I have come to pledge to you upon emigration, and I left my parents weeping. He said: Return to them and make them laugh as you made them weep."
"9067","From al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn Juhadah, he said: I heard al-Hasan saying: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I want to strive. Then he said: Have you any dependent in sin - meaning a near kin needing you? He said: Yes. He said: Then sit with her."
"9068","From al-Thawri, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abu Qilabah, from Muslim ibn Yasar, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a detachment, and with him was a youth who used to take his hand when he stood. Then he asked him to send him in the detachment. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Have you left among your family any aged one? He said: No, except small children. He said: Return to them, for among them is a fine striving."
"9069","From al-Thawri, from Hisham, from al-Hasan, about the two parents when they permit going to raid, he said: If you see their desire is in staying, then stay. And he was asked: What is dutifulness to parents? He said: That you spend for them what you own, and obey them in what they command you, unless it be disobedience to Allah."
"9070","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Yazid, he said: I asked Ubayd Allah ibn Umayr: Does a man raid while his parents dislike that, or one of them? He said: No."
"9071","From Ibn Jurayj, from Muhammad ibn Talhah, that a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I want to raid, and I have come to you to consult you. He said: Have you a mother? He said: Yes. He said: Stay with her, for Paradise is at her foot. Then the second, then the third likewise."
"9072","From Mamar, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade his companions to fight from a mountain side. Then the men turned away from them, and one man remained and fought them. So they shot him and killed him. Then he was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: After we forbade fighting? They said: Yes. Then he left him and did not pray over him."
"9073","From Ibn Uyaynah, from al-Shaybani, he said: I heard a man when al-Jamajim defeated us; then we mentioned him to our companions, and they said: This is al-Marur ibn Suwayd. He said: There was mentioned to Umar a man who left the rank and was killed. Then Umar said: For me to die upon my bed is dearer to me than to fight ahead of a rank; meaning that he left the rank toward the host of the enemy to fight."
"9074","From Mamar, from a man who heard al-Hasan saying: A man said, while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in the rank: Shall I not charge them, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Would you charge to kill them? He said: Yes. He said: Sit until your companions charge."
"9075","From Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: The severest hadith we heard from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - he said: His saying about Sad ibn Muadh, and his saying about the matter of the grave. When it was the expedition of Tabuk, he said: None shall go out with us but a strong man. He said: Then a man went out upon a difficult young camel of his, and it threw him, and he died. Then the people said: A martyr, a martyr. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Bilal to proclaim among the people: No disobedient one shall enter Paradise."
